// Generated from repository Markdown by scripts/docs.ts.
export const articles = [
  {
    "slug": "publication",
    "title": "Publish build variants",
    "html": "<p>Imageway uploads only its own content-addressed <code>imageway/&lt;sha256&gt;.&lt;format&gt;</code> assets. A CDN is a separate service supplied by the operator. No global <code>paths.assets</code> mutation is needed: the Vite plugin rewrites its own artifact URLs, preserving SvelteKit base paths and unrelated assets.</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-ts\">import { imageway } from &#39;svelte-imageway/vite&#39;;\nimport { r2 } from &#39;svelte-imageway/publish/targets&#39;;\nconst target = await r2({ accountId: process.env.CF_ACCOUNT_ID!, bucket: &#39;public-images&#39;,\n  accessKeyEnv: &#39;R2_ACCESS_KEY_ID&#39;, secretKeyEnv: &#39;R2_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY&#39; });\n// In Vite plugins, before sveltekit():\nimageway({ publish: { target, publicBase: &#39;https://images.example.com&#39;,\n  manifestPath: &#39;.imageway/image-manifest.json&#39;, concurrency: 4 } });\n</code></pre>\n<p>Install the optional server SDK: <code>bun add -d @aws-sdk/client-s3</code> for S3/R2/B2, <code>@vercel/blob</code> for Blob or <code>@supabase/storage-js</code> for Supabase. Factory imports are server/build-only. Bunny uses its official HTTP Storage API. SDK methods and response fields are checked against the installed published types. No SDK/credential is serialized into virtual browser configuration.</p>\n<p>Targets: <code>s3({bucket,region,endpoint?,accessKeyEnv?,secretKeyEnv?,forcePathStyle?})</code>, <code>r2</code>, <code>backblaze</code> (S3-compatible options), <code>bunnyStorage({zone,endpoint?,accessKeyEnv?})</code>, <code>vercelBlob({publicBase,tokenEnv?})</code>, <code>supabaseStorage({base,bucket,tokenEnv?})</code>. A custom target implements <code>id</code>, <code>head</code>, <code>put</code>, optional <code>delete</code>. Give credentials write access only to this dedicated asset namespace. Default AWS credential chain supports short-lived CI identities; never expose tokens via Vite <code>VITE_*</code> variables.</p>\n<p><code>dryRun: true</code> performs no remote reads/writes and keeps ordinary local asset URLs. It still emits a planned manifest; that manifest is not proof of an upload. Normal builds use HEAD to skip an existing object with the same hash metadata and size. Stores without hash metadata trust immutable content-addressed keys and size. Preserve this invariant: other tools must not mutate those keys. Transient network/429/5xx failures retry at most twice; authentication failures stop immediately. Failed builds may have uploaded some immutable objects; re-running resumes. The local manifest is replaced atomically only after all uploads succeed. Keep manifests from older deployments.</p>\n<p><code>collectGarbage(previous,current,{target})</code> previews stale keys recorded in the previous manifest. <code>confirm:true</code> permits deletion only of those keys after a default 30-day age check, within the same target and public base. It never lists or clears an entire bucket. Ensure no live deployment still references the old manifest before deleting. There is no automatic GC.</p>\n<p>Configure CDN <code>Cache-Control: public, max-age=31536000, immutable</code> for hashed objects; Bunny Pull Zone caching is configured separately (Storage API does not guarantee edge policy). Supabase uses max-age 31536000, with immutable filenames rather than a promised immutable response directive. Vercel Blob uses explicit public store URLs and no random suffix. Use a public origin that serves exactly the configured base; mismatches fail the build.</p>\n<p>Verified locally: real Vite generation/URL rewriting, manifests, incremental skip, transient retry, permission failure, corrupt metadata, dry-run and safe GC. No live upload was performed without cloud credentials. Account permissions, network policy, egress and billing must be verified in a staging bucket before enabling production publication.</p>\n<p>Sources checked 2026-10-20: <a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AWSJavaScriptSDK/v3/latest/client/s3/\">AWS SDK v3</a>, <a href=\"https://developers.cloudflare.com/r2/api/s3/api/\">R2 S3 compatibility</a>, <a href=\"https://docs.bunny.net/reference/storage-api\">Bunny Storage</a>, <a href=\"https://www.backblaze.com/docs/cloud-storage-s3-compatible-api\">Backblaze S3</a>, <a href=\"https://vercel.com/docs/vercel-blob/using-blob-sdk\">Vercel Blob SDK</a>, <a href=\"https://supabase.com/docs/guides/storage/uploads/standard-uploads\">Supabase standard uploads</a>.</p>\n"
  },
  {
    "slug": "components-and-lcp",
    "title": "Components, art direction and LCP",
    "html": "<p><code>Image</code> and <code>Picture</code> share <code>src</code>, required <code>alt</code>, native img attributes, dimensions, sizes, priority, loading/decoding/fetchpriority, fit/position, provider, placeholder, class/style, onload/onerror and loading/error snippets. Use <code>alt=&quot;&quot;</code> explicitly for decorative images. Localized alt is an ordinary translated string. Svelte 5 runes/snippets only.</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-svelte\">&lt;Picture src={landscape} alt=&quot;A valley&quot; priority\n  sources={[{type:&#39;image/webp&#39;,srcset:portrait.srcset,media:&#39;(max-width: 640px)&#39;,width:portrait.width,height:portrait.height}]}\n  sizes=&quot;sm:100vw md:50vw lg:33vw&quot;&gt;\n  {#snippet error()}&lt;span&gt;Image unavailable&lt;/span&gt;{/snippet}\n&lt;/Picture&gt;\n</code></pre>\n<p><code>priority</code> sets eager/high/sync and one matching preload in svelte:head. The first custom source is the preloaded art-direction source; ensure its media matches the intended LCP viewport. Multiple format preloads would cause unnecessary downloads, so Imageway emits only one. For HTTP Link hints use <code>imagePreloadHandle</code>; set ordinary <code>Link: &lt;...&gt;; rel=preload; as=image</code> before headers stream. Portable Kit hooks cannot promise HTTP 103 Early Hints; platform support is operator-specific.</p>\n<p>Images reserve intrinsic dimensions/aspect-ratio. Use CSS that preserves those dimensions when changing layouts. <code>sizes=&quot;auto, 100vw&quot;</code> is passed only for lazy images, with explicit fallback for older browsers; eager images use the fallback. Never lazy-load the expected LCP image. Client Hints are not automatically requested: arbitrary Width/DPR/Vary values can explode cache cardinality. Runtime DPR is explicit and bounded; Save-Data lowers quality after authorization and participates in response/cache variation.</p>\n<p>Placeholders use inline CSS and remain useful without JS. Reduced motion disables the reveal. A cached/304 image does not restart a fade. Cross-origin timing without Timing-Allow-Origin may omit transfer data; Imageway then keeps the image visible instead of assuming it should animate. <code>contentVisibility=&quot;auto&quot;</code> and <code>transitionName=&quot;hero-image&quot;</code> are optional; choose valid stable names and avoid duplicate View Transition names on a page.</p>\n<p><code>getImageProps</code> returns native attributes plus <code>sources</code> and placeholder; remove nonnative fields before spreading onto img. <code>BackgroundImage</code> emits ordinary URL fallback and image-set, with optional media variants. <code>ImageMeta</code> emits OG/Twitter/image_src metadata; supply an absolute public origin for relative artifacts. ImageConfigProvider isolates reactive subtree settings, including SSR requests.</p>\n<p>The full Image entry currently exceeds the 1.5 KiB gzip target; see the measured bundle report and issue 0001. Headless markup avoids component state when an application does not need snippets/transitions.</p>\n"
  },
  {
    "slug": "cli",
    "title": "CLI",
    "html": "<p>Use <code>bun x svelte-imageway --help</code> (or <code>npx svelte-imageway</code> after publication). Node 22.17+ and Bun are supported. Output is plain text, respects <code>NO_COLOR</code> without ANSI escapes, and contains no telemetry. <code>--json</code> produces JSON; audit findings exit 1, command/configuration errors exit 2, success exits 0.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><code>init [dir] [--dry-run]</code>: requires an existing Svelte/Vite project. Adds the plugin to a single literal <code>plugins: [...]</code> array using the TypeScript parser, preserving comments and other options. Computed/multi-config arrays require manual integration. Adds ambient types and a complete local SVG example without overwriting existing files. A second run is idempotent. Review the diff and run the host build.</li>\n<li><code>optimize [dir] [--output dir] [--width 1920] [--quality 75] [--format webp] [--dry-run] [--watch]</code>: bounded Sharp worker pipeline, writes hashed variants and a JSON report; originals are preserved. Animation is explicitly flattened for this batch command. Runtime limits and codec availability apply. Dry-run reports source sizes without decoding/writing; it is not proof an image will decode. Width/quality follow the runtime ladder. Watch uses the Node filesystem watcher, debounces events and skips generated output/cache. Re-run a clean batch after missed operating-system watcher events.</li>\n<li><code>audit [dir] [--json]</code>: parses Svelte and scans Markdown/CSS/large source files. Reports missing alt/dimensions/loading, likely first-image LCP review, legacy formats, heavy images and unoptimized backgrounds. Dynamic spreads and LCP identification are review hints, not browser measurements. Explicit decorative <code>alt=&quot;&quot;</code> is valid; Markdown empty alt requires human review.</li>\n<li><code>deploy cloudflare|docker [--output dir] [--dry-run]</code>: generates standalone configuration and instructions only. Never logs in, creates buckets, enables billing or executes deployment. Review names, binding availability, secret variables and costs first. Existing files are preserved. Docker template references 0.1.0: before publication, substitute the locally packed artifact. Cloudflare <code>LOCAL_OFFLINE=false</code> is production mode; use true only for the local basic-transform emulator. Sources default private.</li>\n<li><code>purge --endpoint https://your-worker/purge --key images/a.png --yes</code> (or one <code>--prefix</code>/<code>--tag</code>): token from <code>IMAGEWAY_PURGE_TOKEN</code>, customizable with <code>--token-env</code>. No token arguments or redirect following. This command targets Imageway&#39;s protected Worker API, not undocumented vendor APIs or Node&#39;s image route. Purge may incur new transformation costs.</li>\n<li><code>migrate [dir] --from enhanced-img|imagetools [--write]</code>: preview by default. Writes <code>.imageway-backup</code> before mutation; refuses to overwrite a backup. Parses imports/Svelte syntax. Converts supported enhanced imports and self-closing static/imported enhanced markup with explicit alt. Unknown directives, dynamic markup and imagetools object consumers need review. <code>as=picture</code> imports become typed Imageway artifacts, whose field shape differs; update consumers to <code>&lt;Image src={artifact}&gt;</code>. Run <code>init</code>, remove the old plugin after reviewing the diff, then build/test. This is a conservative codemod, not a guarantee of arbitrary application migration.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>No command deletes source media. Deploy does not reserve infrastructure. Publish/GC are the explicit build APIs documented separately.</p>\n"
  },
  {
    "slug": "remote-and-cms",
    "title": "Remote images and CMS",
    "html": "<pre><code class=\"language-svelte\">&lt;script lang=&quot;ts&quot;&gt;\n  import { Image } from &#39;svelte-imageway&#39;;\n&lt;/script&gt;\n&lt;Image src=&quot;photos/trail.jpg&quot; width={1200} height={800} alt=&quot;A mountain trail&quot; /&gt;\n</code></pre>\n<p>Select the public provider in central Vite config; the component stays the same. <code>providers: { &#39;images.example.com&#39;: providerA, &#39;*&#39;: providerB }</code> selects by exact source hostname. An explicit <code>provider</code> prop or ImageConfigProvider subtree overrides the central choice. Only the supported capability intersection is portable: advanced native transforms may be unavailable, and fail explicitly. Remote intrinsic dimensions are supplied by the application/CMS; the browser is not sent a Sharp metadata probe.</p>\n<p>Use <code>cms({kind,base})</code> from <code>svelte-imageway/cms</code> or the CMS provider subpath. <code>recognizeCms(url)</code> recognizes exact public Sanity, Contentful, Storyblok, Prismic, DatoCMS, Shopify and Unsplash hosts. Directus needs an explicit hint/base because it is self-hosted. Preserve Unsplash attribution and fulfill the upstream API&#39;s attribution obligations. Signed third-party query URLs are not freely editable: regenerate them with the vendor server signer.</p>\n<p>Strapi and WordPress return pre-generated variants. Map each variant to <code>{url,width,height,mime}</code> and call <code>cmsArtifact(original, variants, origin)</code>. WordPress <code>source_url</code> maps to <code>url</code>; Strapi <code>formats</code> entries already contain dimensions. There is no fictitious <code>?w=</code> API for arbitrary CMS installs.</p>\n<p>For the local Node service, remote sources require an exact HTTPS origin allowlist and DNS-pinned fetch. Private IPs, metadata endpoints, redirects outside policy, oversized sources and traversal fail closed. Worker mode uses approved R2 keys instead of a remote proxy. An HMAC signature is required for arbitrary transformations; never put signing secrets into frontend props.</p>\n<p><code>createFallback({providers,transport,original?})</code> is a server-only circuit breaker. The transport must enforce origin/byte policy. Timeout/network/5xx can advance to a backup; auth, validation and budget failures cannot. Original fallback requires an explicit authorizing callback. No JavaScript retry loop races multiple CDN downloads in the browser.</p>\n"
  },
  {
    "slug": "faq-and-migration",
    "title": "FAQ, troubleshooting and migration",
    "html": "<p><strong>Does the package include a global CDN?</strong> It provides image artifacts, provider adapters and a deployable origin. A CDN/storage subscription is supplied by the operator.</p>\n<p><strong>Why is my transform rejected?</strong> Check runtime vs build directive names, locked preset values, width/quality ladders, allowed origins, actual codec inventory and provider capability subset. Detailed safe errors are returned; debug logs exclude IP/source secrets.</p>\n<p><strong>Why does a dynamic URL have no srcset?</strong> A URL alone cannot select a CDN or discover intrinsic dimensions safely in the browser. Configure a provider and supply dimensions. Local <code>?img</code> imports work without a provider.</p>\n<p><strong>Why is Sharp missing?</strong> Native optional installation may be unsupported or disabled by your package manager. Install a compatible Sharp build with Bun, check codecCapabilities, or choose a native provider. Do not import server entries into a browser bundle.</p>\n<p><strong>Why does GIF fail locally in the Worker?</strong> The offline Images emulator cannot encode GIF and supports a smaller operation set; it returns 422. WebP/JPEG/PNG/AVIF basics are executed locally. Production advanced features need an account-backed staging test.</p>\n<p><strong>Why did a signed URL stop working?</strong> Expiry/key rotation/preset revision or normalized parameters changed. Sign the materialized canonical v1 request with the intended endpoint and policy revision; never append transforms after signing.</p>\n<p><strong>Why is a CMS URL signature rejected?</strong> Vendor signatures are not Imageway signatures. Use a vendor server signer after native transforms or deliver through an independently authorized Imageway key.</p>\n<p><strong>How do I migrate?</strong> Run <code>migrate --from enhanced-img</code> or <code>--from imagetools</code> without --write, inspect planned changes/review items, then apply with backups and run init. Imagetools picture object shapes differ; convert consumers to Image src. Remove the old plugin after the new build passes. Restore from .imageway-backup when reviewing a failed migration. The CLI never rewrites unsupported operations blindly.</p>\n<p><strong>Why no 1.0 label?</strong> Gzip size, benchmark parity, exotic codecs and live cloud/CI verification are measurable acceptance requirements. The report keeps failures and unverified infrastructure visible. 0.1 is for evaluation; reaching 1.0 requires resolving those gates.</p>\n"
  },
  {
    "slug": "api-and-recipes",
    "title": "API reference and integration recipes",
    "html": "<h2 id=\"package-entries\">Package entries</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><code>svelte-imageway</code>: Image, Picture, BackgroundImage, ImageMeta, ImageConfigProvider, headless/context/types.</li>\n<li><code>/props</code>: getImageProps and imageSizes, no Svelte runtime dependency.</li>\n<li><code>/core</code>: parseRequest, normalizeRequest, imageUrl, canonicalQuery, cacheIdentity, signRequest, verifyRequest, negotiateFormat, mimeType, defineConfig.</li>\n<li><code>/config</code> and <code>/types</code>: Node-safe configuration and ambient import declarations.</li>\n<li><code>/vite</code>: imageway plugin, parseDirectives, generateImageTypes.</li>\n<li><code>/kit</code>: imageAdapter, imageStrategy, imageHandle, imagePreloadHandle, createImageService.</li>\n<li><code>/node</code>: Sharp processing/cache/source helpers and actual codec inventory.</li>\n<li><code>/edge</code>: createImageWorker, ImageBudget, cfImageTransform and binding types.</li>\n<li><code>/providers/&lt;name&gt;</code>: independent credential-free factories; custom providers implement name, capabilities, buildUrl and validate.</li>\n<li><code>/cms</code>: recognizeCms, cms, cmsArtifact.</li>\n<li><code>/providers/fallback</code>: required-policy server transport orchestration.</li>\n<li><code>/publish</code> and <code>/publish/targets</code>: StorageTarget, publishAssets, manifests, explicit GC and optional SDK factories.</li>\n<li><code>/cli</code>: main() with stable process exit codes; binary also available as svelte-imageway.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Public TypeScript declarations are the exact reference for option shapes. The versioned URL spec has JSON Schema and OpenAPI. Config schema describes serializable fields; executable provider factories and credentialed storage targets belong in typed TypeScript configuration, not JSON.</p>\n<h2 id=\"custom-provider\">Custom provider</h2>\n<pre><code class=\"language-ts\">const provider = {\n  name: &#39;my-cdn&#39;,\n  capabilities: {formats:[&#39;webp&#39;,&#39;jpeg&#39;],fit:[&#39;inside&#39;],smartCrop:false,animation:false,signature:false},\n  validate() {},\n  buildUrl({key,w,q,format}) { return `https://images.example/${encodeURIComponent(key!)}?w=${w}&amp;q=${q}&amp;format=${format}`; }\n};\n</code></pre>\n<p>Use a custom object explicitly in a subtree/component or <code>generic({template,capabilities})</code> for central Vite serialization. The custom provider is responsible for rejecting unsupported transforms and enforcing its own remote account policy. Do not put secrets into the factory.</p>\n<h2 id=\"ecosystem-recipes\">Ecosystem recipes</h2>\n<p>Tailwind: pass class attributes normally and retain aspect-ratio/dimension layout. Storybook/Vitest browser: include imageway() in that Vite instance. Standalone Svelte needs imageway() for the public virtual config. Vitest pure headless code can import /props without the virtual module.</p>\n<p>mdsvex/Markdown: use a Svelte Image component with a typed imported artifact, or a renderer that supplies explicit dimensions. No automatic raw img preprocessor is shipped. The CLI audit helps identify Markdown images needing review.</p>\n<p>PWA: cache public content-hashed build variants with an immutable asset strategy. Do not precache signed/private URLs or strip query authorization fields. Purge old service-worker versions before garbage-collecting objects referenced by them. Browser storage quota and offline consent are application concerns.</p>\n<p>Sitemap: list absolute public original/canonical image URLs with application page ownership and rights metadata. Imageway does not infer a complete image sitemap from component runtime state. Video conversion, image sitemap generation, automatic overlay, avatar/grid helpers and Storybook package distribution remain planned bonuses, not shipped APIs.</p>\n"
  },
  {
    "slug": "getting-started",
    "title": "Getting started",
    "html": "<p>Imageway is a Svelte 5 image toolkit with three delivery layers: build artifacts, native providers and a self-hosted image service. Version 0.1.0 is a local preview; the public npm name has been checked but no release is published yet. Use the packed artifact until the maintainer publishes <code>next</code>.</p>\n<h2 id=\"three-steps\">Three steps</h2>\n<ol>\n<li>Install <code>bun add svelte-imageway@next</code> after publication, or <code>bun add /absolute/path/svelte-imageway-0.1.0.tgz</code> for this checkout. Sharp is an optional native dependency: local imports need a supported Sharp build; managed providers do not.</li>\n<li>Add <code>imageway()</code> before <code>sveltekit()</code> in <code>vite.config.ts</code>:</li>\n</ol>\n<pre><code class=\"language-ts\">import { imageway } from &#39;svelte-imageway/vite&#39;;\n// preserve your existing adapter and other plugins\nexport default defineConfig({ plugins: [imageway(), sveltekit()] });\n</code></pre>\n<ol start=\"3\">\n<li>Import a local artifact and render an image:</li>\n</ol>\n<pre><code class=\"language-svelte\">&lt;script lang=&quot;ts&quot;&gt;\n  import { Image } from &#39;svelte-imageway&#39;;\n  import hero from &#39;./hero.jpg?img&#39;;\n&lt;/script&gt;\n&lt;Image src={hero} alt=&quot;A trail through the mountains&quot; priority sizes=&quot;100vw&quot; /&gt;\n</code></pre>\n<p><code>bun x svelte-imageway init</code> can prepare the plugin, ambient types and an example in a literal Vite configuration. No remote provider or account is required for local imports. Build output contains hashed images; use immutable caching on these assets.</p>\n<h2 id=\"central-configuration\">Central configuration</h2>\n<pre><code class=\"language-ts\">// svelte-imageway.config.ts\nimport { defineConfig } from &#39;svelte-imageway/config&#39;;\nimport { cloudinary } from &#39;svelte-imageway/providers/cloudinary&#39;;\nexport default defineConfig({ provider: cloudinary({ cloudName: &#39;your-public-cloud&#39; }),\n  widths: [320,640,960,1280,1920], qualities: [50,65,75,85,95], quality: 75 });\n</code></pre>\n<p>Import that object into Vite and pass <code>imageway({config})</code>. Runtime hooks may import the same object and pass it as <code>config</code>; source roots, cache directories, signing environment variable names and publication targets are explicit server/build options. They are excluded from the browser-safe virtual configuration. This package deliberately does not execute an arbitrary config file automatically inside the browser or CLI.</p>\n<p>SvelteKit 3 places adapter settings in <code>sveltekit({adapter})</code> and TypeScript projects extend <code>$app/tsconfig</code>. Preserve the configuration produced by your installed official Svelte CLI. Use <code>svelte-imageway/config</code> in Node-loaded configuration; importing the main component entry there would require a Svelte loader.</p>\n<h2 id=\"choose-a-delivery-layer\">Choose a delivery layer</h2>\n<p>Build-time local imports are the zero-account default. For dynamic URL strings, configure a provider and intrinsic dimensions. For private originals, sign requests on the server and keep public caching disabled. For a static site on a CDN, publish build variants rather than adding runtime transformation.</p>\n"
  },
  {
    "slug": "local-images",
    "title": "Local images and formats",
    "html": "<p>Typed imports return an ImageArtifact: <code>src</code>, <code>srcset</code>, <code>sources</code>, intrinsic width/height/aspectRatio, optional placeholder/dominantColor/hash.</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-ts\">import hero from &#39;./hero.jpg?img&amp;w=400;800;1200&amp;format=avif;webp;jpg&amp;q=75&amp;placeholder=lqip&#39;;\n</code></pre>\n<p>Widths/qualities on build imports are exact, deduplicated and clamped to intrinsic width. Runtime requests use bounded ladders. Limit one import to 64 variants. Supported placeholder strategies: lqip (default, under 1 KB), color, none, thumbhash, blurhash. Hashes are decoded at build time into a browser-displayable preview; no client decoder is shipped.</p>\n<p>Dev transforms are lazy: import metadata first, encode only when the browser requests a variant. Build transforms use a persistent content/codec/policy cache in <code>node_modules/.cache/svelte-imageway</code> and shared bounded worker admission. Content-hashed old URLs remain valid during HMR, with bounded retention of dev jobs.</p>\n<p>JPEG, PNG, WebP, AVIF, TIFF, approved SVG and GIF inputs are exercised with real fixtures. JPEG uses mozjpeg. Auto orientation removes EXIF/GPS. <code>metadata=icc</code> retains ICC without retaining GPS; CMYK is converted for web display. Alpha build inputs replace an unsuitable JPEG fallback with PNG. Animated inputs preserve WebP/GIF, and omit formats whose encoder would silently flatten them. <code>animation=first</code> deliberately creates a still. Animated AVIF/video conversion is not implemented.</p>\n<p>HEIC/HEIF and JXL depend on the actual Sharp/libvips distribution and fail with an actionable codec error if absent. BMP/ICO and WASM fallback decoders are not implemented in this preview. Inspect <code>codecCapabilities()</code> from <code>svelte-imageway/node</code>; do not infer codec support from the filename. This limitation remains part of the 1.0 acceptance backlog.</p>\n<p>SVG accepts only a small geometric dialect and is validated before SVGO/Sharp. Scripts, references, text, styles and external resources are rejected. For rich illustrations, rasterize with a trusted design tool rather than relaxing the runtime policy.</p>\n"
  },
  {
    "slug": "adapters-and-runtime",
    "title": "Adapters and runtime",
    "html": "<p>Wrap the actual adapter object to preserve all official adapter Vite/runtime hooks:</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-ts\">import node from &#39;@sveltejs/adapter-node&#39;;\nimport { imageAdapter } from &#39;svelte-imageway/kit&#39;;\n// Inside sveltekit configuration:\nsveltekit({adapter: imageAdapter(node())});\n</code></pre>\n<p><code>imageAdapter(&#39;node&#39;|&#39;static&#39;|&#39;cloudflare&#39;|&#39;vercel&#39;|&#39;netlify&#39;)</code> dynamically imports the selected official adapter (install it first). For adapter-auto, resolve the actual deployment target explicitly rather than guessing from an unknown environment. A supplied Bun/Deno adapter needs an explicit matching target and verified runtime; there is no invented official Bun/Deno adapter package.</p>\n<p>Static: generate imports at build time, optionally publish them directly to CDN storage. Dynamic URL strings need a real external provider on static sites. Node/Bun: use the Sharp service with sourceRoot, signing policy and disk cache. Cloudflare/Vercel/Netlify: native providers are the default; local Sharp on an incompatible edge target is rejected with a clear error. Native deployment entitlement and external origin allowlisting remain platform configuration.</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-ts\">// src/hooks.server.ts\nimport { imageHandle } from &#39;svelte-imageway/kit&#39;;\nexport const handle = imageHandle({ sourceRoot: &#39;/approved/images&#39;,\n  publicSources: false, signingKeys: { current: &#39;IMAGEWAY_SIGNING_KEY&#39; } });\n</code></pre>\n<p>Compose with existing hooks using SvelteKit sequence. The handler only intercepts the configured image route and leaves prerender/streaming SSR resolution intact. It never buffers arbitrary streamed page HTML or mutates transformPageChunk output. Place preload hints before the response is committed; a hook cannot retrofit 103 after streaming starts.</p>\n<p>Verified apps: basic Kit, adapter-static, adapter-node, local Wrangler bindings, standalone Node/Bun and Docker. Official Cloudflare/Vercel/Netlify adapter wrappers are constructed/type-checked and their native URLs have conformance tests. Cloud account deployment has not been executed. Deno Deploy and Vercel/Netlify Edge use their native providers; this preview does not ship an unmeasured Sharp/WASM edge runtime.</p>\n<p>Cache defaults private. Explicit public mutable originals use 60 seconds plus SWR; verified content-hash URLs may be immutable. Signed public URLs clamp TTL to expiration with must-revalidate. Signatures do not make a mutable source immutable. See the threat model and topology guide before sharing cache across tenants.</p>\n"
  },
  {
    "slug": "url-contract",
    "title": "Universal URL contract: v1 design draft",
    "html": "<p>Status: v1 parser, signer, JSON Schema and OpenAPI implemented; pre-release compatibility is covered by executable contract tests. Platform policy and network checks remain separate from structural schemas. Unknown or unsupported operations fail explicitly.</p>\n<h2 id=\"endpoint-and-sources\">Endpoint and sources</h2>\n<p>Canonical service endpoint: <code>/_img/v1</code>. The Kit integration may offer <code>/_img</code> as a compatibility entry, but generated URLs include the version. Only GET and HEAD image retrieval are in this contract; purge/metrics/health are separate authenticated or explicitly exposed endpoints.</p>\n<p>Exactly one source is required:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><code>url</code>: absolute HTTPS URL on an explicit origin allowlist. Reject credentials, fragments, unsafe ports and disallowed destinations at every redirect/connection. Source query content remains intact.</li>\n<li><code>key</code>: opaque object key resolved inside a configured source namespace. It is not an arbitrary local filesystem path. Resolve local keys with realpath containment; reject traversal and symlink escapes.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>No wildcard public proxy is a default. A component&#39;s unrecognized remote URL may remain an unoptimized public URL; requesting a transformation requires a configured provider/security policy.</p>\n<h2 id=\"parameter-vocabulary\">Parameter vocabulary</h2>\n<p>All ranges below are proposed safe defaults, configurable only server-side within backend limits. Machine schemas must express the structural constraints; semantic/preset/network constraints require runtime checks.</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Parameter</th>\n<th>Type / values</th>\n<th>Proposed semantics</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td><code>url</code> / <code>key</code></td>\n<td>string, mutually exclusive</td>\n<td>Source identity; source URL preserves signed query semantics</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>w</code>, <code>h</code></td>\n<td>integer 1–4096</td>\n<td>Logical target box dimensions; at least one dimension or a preset is needed for dynamic transforms</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>dpr</code></td>\n<td>1, 1.5, 2, 3</td>\n<td>Multiply logical dimensions, with decoded/output limits checked on physical pixels</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>q</code></td>\n<td>integer 1–100</td>\n<td>Quantized quality ladder; quality scales differ by encoder/provider</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>format</code></td>\n<td><code>avif</code>, <code>webp</code>, <code>jpeg</code>, <code>png</code>, <code>gif</code>, <code>svg</code>, <code>auto</code></td>\n<td>Canonical <code>jpeg</code>; import-side <code>jpg</code> is normalized to <code>jpeg</code>; SVG restricted to approved processing paths</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>fit</code></td>\n<td><code>cover</code>, <code>contain</code>, <code>fill</code>, <code>inside</code>, <code>outside</code>, <code>scale-down</code></td>\n<td>Transform semantics, separate from CSS object-fit; provider support checked</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>position</code></td>\n<td><code>center</code>, cardinal/corner anchors, <code>attention</code>, <code>entropy</code></td>\n<td>Crop focus; smart crop is capability-gated</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>blur</code></td>\n<td>number 0–20</td>\n<td>Zero disables; positive sigma/backend mapping must be documented</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>sharpen</code></td>\n<td>number 0–10</td>\n<td>Zero disables; map only where semantics are supported</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>bg</code></td>\n<td><code>transparent</code> or lowercase 6/8-digit hex without <code>#</code></td>\n<td>Padding/flattening color, including alpha if supported</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>rotate</code></td>\n<td>0, 90, 180, 270</td>\n<td>Clockwise rotation after EXIF auto-orientation</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>flip</code></td>\n<td><code>none</code>, <code>horizontal</code>, <code>vertical</code>, <code>both</code></td>\n<td>Explicit axis mapping; no ambiguous vendor flip names</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>metadata</code></td>\n<td><code>none</code>, <code>icc</code>, <code>safe</code></td>\n<td>Default none; ICC preserves color profile only. Safe metadata allowlist must exclude GPS and identifying EXIF</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>animation</code></td>\n<td><code>preserve</code>, <code>first</code></td>\n<td>Default preserve when supported; do not silently flatten animation</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>preset</code></td>\n<td><code>thumb</code>, <code>card</code>, <code>hero</code>, <code>og</code>, configured safe name</td>\n<td>Server-defined restricted recipe with versioned policy</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>rev</code></td>\n<td>restricted string</td>\n<td>Trusted source revision/content identity; arbitrary user text alone does not prove immutable content</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>kid</code></td>\n<td>restricted public key identifier</td>\n<td>Select configured HMAC key; never a key or arbitrary environment-variable name</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>exp</code></td>\n<td>integer Unix seconds</td>\n<td>Required when signing; bounded maximum lifetime and configured clock skew</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>sig</code></td>\n<td>unpadded base64url HMAC-SHA256</td>\n<td>Server-generated signature over the canonical signing payload</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<p>Omitted defaults normalize to explicit internal values; generated URLs consistently materialize and include stable defaults. Unknown query names, duplicate keys, NaN, Infinity, empty required fields, invalid UTF-8/percent encodings and noncanonical ambiguous boolean spellings are rejected. Never coerce a malformed value to a permissive default.</p>\n<p>Proposed width ladder: 320, 480, 640, 768, 960, 1280, 1600, 1920, 2560. Proposed quality ladder: 50, 65, 75, 85, 95. Defaults: quality 75, DPR 1, fit inside, position center, no metadata. Exact ladders and final range enforcement must be fixed by M1 tests. Static source dimensions clamp generated variants without upscaling.</p>\n<h2 id=\"normalization-algorithm\">Normalization algorithm</h2>\n<ol>\n<li>Parse a single query representation with strict encoding validation; reject duplicate and unknown fields before building a map.</li>\n<li>Validate source syntax, source namespace and bounds. Network resolution/connection validation remains mandatory at fetch time.</li>\n<li>Expand the selected preset using its immutable policy revision. Reject overrides outside its locked fields/ranges.</li>\n<li>Canonicalize enum aliases, decimal number spelling, background color and logical width/quality buckets. Use the smallest permitted bucket greater than or equal to the requested value; reject values above the configured maximum. Enforce physical dimensions and total pixels after DPR.</li>\n<li>Keep the upstream source URL&#39;s query sequence, encoding meaning and credentials policy intact. Encode it once as a query value; never recursively decode it. Do not reorder its signed parameters.</li>\n<li>Sort canonical outer parameter names by ASCII order. Percent-encode UTF-8 bytes using RFC 3986 unreserved characters, <code>%20</code> for spaces, uppercase hexadecimal escapes. Emit each key exactly once.</li>\n<li>Sign or verify the payload described below. Validate authorization and expiry before cache lookup.</li>\n<li>Resolve <code>auto</code> against supported encoders and Accept preferences, respecting media-range q=0 and q-values. Save-Data may select only a bounded, policy-approved quality bucket; Client Hints are opt-in and similarly bucketed.</li>\n<li>Derive the transform cache identity from source namespace/tenant, verified revision or revalidation policy, normalized transformations, negotiated output MIME, preset/pipeline/codec version and metadata policy. Auth fields can be omitted from stored transform identity only after authorization succeeds and isolation is preserved.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>The canonical byte string, normalized object and cache identity are distinct concepts with shared normalization. No locale-aware sorting, floating-point string surprises or raw Accept strings belong in cache identity. M1 must supply byte-level expected vectors for this algorithm.</p>\n<h2 id=\"signatures-and-expiry\">Signatures and expiry</h2>\n<p>Proposed signing payload is four UTF-8 lines without a trailing newline: method <code>GET</code>, normalized endpoint path, protocol/policy identifier <code>v1:&lt;preset revision&gt;</code> (or <code>v1:-</code> without a preset), sorted encoded request excluding <code>sig</code>. It includes <code>kid</code>, <code>exp</code>, source, all output-affecting fields and relevant preset revision. HEAD is authorized using the same GET payload and cannot change the output contract. Use HMAC-SHA256, unpadded base64url output and constant-time comparison over fixed-size decoded bytes.</p>\n<p>Reject missing/unknown key IDs, invalid signature encoding/length, expired or implausibly distant expiry, duplicate parameters and altered values. Configure current/previous key IDs for rotation with bounded overlap. Error responses and logs must not print secrets or private source query credentials.</p>\n<p>Private content defaults to private/no-store; a CDN must not serve it past access expiry without validating at the edge. Public signed transform URLs may share decoded artifacts if policy allows, but signing alone does not justify <code>immutable</code>.</p>\n<h2 id=\"format-modes-and-cache-semantics\">Format modes and cache semantics</h2>\n<p>Explicit format is recommended for build assets, <code>&lt;picture&gt;</code> sources, preloads and most CDN configurations. MIME must match the actual encoder output; do not declare <code>image/avif</code> and serve WebP bytes as an invisible fallback.</p>\n<p>Negotiated <code>format=auto</code> chooses among supported, acceptable encoders, with AVIF/WebP preferred only when allowed by Accept and platform limits. Include output format in internal cache identity and send <code>Vary: Accept</code>. If Save-Data or hints affect output, either redirect/rewrite to an explicit canonical variant or vary on the exact bounded inputs at every downstream cache.</p>\n<p>Content-addressed public build assets can use <code>public, max-age=31536000, immutable</code>. Mutable remote sources use bounded TTL, validators and optional stale-while-revalidate. ETags identify output bytes. Implement correct conditional GET/HEAD and 304 behavior. Byte ranges are advertised only for finalized cached bytes where correct range responses are implemented; otherwise use <code>Accept-Ranges: none</code> or omit it.</p>\n<p>See <a href=\"/docs/research-platforms-and-costs#negotiated-cache-configuration\">CDN-specific cache guidance and pending verification</a>.</p>\n<h2 id=\"error-model\">Error model</h2>\n<p>Proposed errors: 400 malformed request, 401 invalid/missing required signature, 403 disallowed origin/preset, 404 missing approved key, 406 no acceptable supported format, 413 byte/pixel/output limit, 415 unsupported/unsafe input, 422 unsupported transformation capability, 429 rate/budget limit, 502 approved origin/decoder failure and 504 timeout. Never fall back across a security rejection.</p>\n<p>Machine-readable errors should use a stable code and an actionable public message without source secrets or stack traces. Correlate with a nonidentifying request ID and optional structured logs.</p>\n<h2 id=\"presets-and-variant-bounds\">Presets and variant bounds</h2>\n<p>Proposed preset purposes: thumb (small square crop), card (bounded responsive content), hero (larger responsive image), og (1200×630 social output). Store their exact dimensions/format/quality/override policy in the central config; do not bake conflicting definitions into providers.</p>\n<p>Bound widths, qualities, DPR, crop policies, animation pages, simultaneous jobs and queued requests. The variant cardinality test must compute the product of allowed combinations for each exposed preset, prove deduplication of equivalent physical output dimensions, and reject arbitrary dimensions. LRU eviction alone does not prevent variant-generation abuse.</p>\n<h2 id=\"m1-conformance-deliverables\">M1 conformance deliverables</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Markdown plus JSON Schema and OpenAPI for request/error shapes.</li>\n<li>Canonicalization, reserved characters, signed upstream URLs, aliases and duplicate-key vectors.</li>\n<li>HMAC/expiry/key-rotation vectors and verification-before-cache regression tests.</li>\n<li>Accept q-value parsing and format-key isolation vectors.</li>\n<li>Preset locks, DPR bounds, variant cardinality and pipeline-version cache invalidation tests.</li>\n<li>Node source acquisition tests covering traversal, encoded IPs, DNS rebinding, redirects, body limits and timeout.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>No schema or wire-compatibility promise is made until those deliverables are executed and reviewed.</p>\n"
  },
  {
    "slug": "security",
    "title": "Security policy and threat model",
    "html": "<p>This is a 0.1.0 preview. Do not deploy an open unrestricted optimizer. A package is not a managed security perimeter or a CDN. Review deployment limits and trust boundaries before exposing the service.</p>\n<h2 id=\"reporting\">Reporting</h2>\n<p>No public repository/security contact has been configured yet. Until a maintainer adds their verified private security channel, do not publish exploit details or credentials in public issues. The release procedure requires a repository with private vulnerability reporting enabled. Supported preview line: latest 0.1.x; fixes may change behavior that relied on unsafe inputs. Production security review remains an operator responsibility.</p>\n<h2 id=\"assets-attackers-and-trust\">Assets, attackers and trust</h2>\n<p>Protect source confidentiality, filesystem contents, origin credentials, CPU/memory and paid image transformations. Attackers may control image query strings and uploaded media. Operators control source roots, allowlists, provider configuration, signing keys, storage credentials, cache directories and proxy/CDN rules. Source-root and cache-directory writers are trusted: Node&#39;s realpath + final O_NOFOLLOW does not sandbox a hostile concurrent filesystem administrator changing parent directories. Restrict filesystem ownership, mount sources read-only and isolate the service/container.</p>\n<h2 id=\"source-policy\">Source policy</h2>\n<p>Node remote fetch requires exact HTTPS origin allowlisting, port 443, no URL credentials/hash. Every redirect is reauthorized, at most three. All A/AAAA results must be public; IPv4-mapped IPv6, private/reserved/link-local/metadata ranges are blocked. The vetted address is pinned into the actual TLS lookup while preserving the original hostname/certificate check. DNS, transport and stream read share a deadline. Cookies, Authorization and client headers are not forwarded. Responses use identity encoding and bounded bytes; input MIME is determined from content. Do not replace the transport with an unchecked ambient fetch.</p>\n<p>The Worker accepts approved R2 key prefixes only. It cannot guarantee DNS pinning in Workers fetch, so it does not expose an arbitrary remote-image proxy. Optional cf.image uses an operator-owned public origin serving the same immutable keys and refuses redirects; that origin-to-R2 content equivalence is an explicit operator trust boundary.</p>\n<p>Local keys reject traversal, absolute paths, empty/dot segments and non-ASCII path metacharacters. realpath must remain under the configured root; the final open uses O_NOFOLLOW. Missing or oversized sources fail closed.</p>\n<h2 id=\"authorization-and-cache\">Authorization and cache</h2>\n<p>Unsigned requests need a locked preset by default. Arbitrary transforms require server-generated HMAC-SHA256 (32-byte minimum secret), expiration and key ID. Rotate with current/previous environment keys; remove previous only after existing links expire. A present invalid signature field is never treated as an unsigned request. Authorization is verified before shared-cache reuse. Preset revisions and normalized defaults participate in the signature and representation identity. Use short lifetimes; maximum verification lifetime is 24 hours by default. Never put secrets in VITE_* variables or browser config.</p>\n<p>Private is the response default (<code>private, no-store</code>). Only explicitly public sources may use public cache headers. Signed public responses clamp max-age to the remaining signature lifetime and omit stale-while-revalidate/immutable; a CDN must honor that TTL and not serve stale past expiry. Hash-revision unsigned public URLs may be immutable only after verifying the input content hash. Signature alone is not content immutability. The CDN cache key must retain all transform/source/query authorization fields; do not strip signature fields at the external CDN. Internal format cache keys normalize Accept/Save-Data and exclude authorization only after origin verification. Do not share a cache across tenants with different source authorization policies unless using an explicit namespace and equivalent policy.</p>\n<p>ETag does not authorize access. Invalid/expired requests cannot get 304. Negative errors are no-store. Purge and metrics use separate 32-byte bearer secrets with constant-time HMAC/timing-safe checks. Logs contain code/status/duration, not client IP or source query. Purge affects origin generations; CDN copies need their own purge or expiry. Logical Worker selector history is bounded (64 distinct selectors); plan broad stable prefixes/tags, and perform an explicit namespace/cache maintenance reset if capacity is reached.</p>\n<h2 id=\"decoder-resource-policy\">Decoder/resource policy</h2>\n<p>Sharp runs in bounded worker_threads with shared admission across imports/processors, queue byte/count limits, input/output bytes, physical dimensions, total decoded pixels/frames, frame count &lt;=100, timeout/worker termination, EXIF/GPS stripping and opt-in ICC only. Node service caps concurrent requests at eight. Workers default to 8 MB input/output, 16 MP total decoded frames, one transform at a time and at most 16 queued tasks. Worker GIF/WebP/APNG container frame counts are inspected before native decode; AVIF sequences are rejected. Native cloud operations may continue after a response timeout: admission guards limit accepted work, not an account-wide dollar spend. Set provider billing alerts and platform limits separately. Native allocations are not limited by JavaScript heap settings; container memory/CPU limits are the final boundary. Unsupported exotic codecs fail explicitly; no automatic unbounded WASM decoder is used.</p>\n<p>SVG is a strict geometry-only XML dialect, validated with a pinned SAX parser. No namespaces/prefixed active elements, scripts, foreignObject, external/internal references, CSS escapes, styles, event handlers, text, DTD/entities, processing instructions or CDATA. Depth &lt;=32, elements &lt;=10,000. Runtime raw SVG output is disabled; approved build SVG can be optimized and emitted as an image. Serve SVG on a cookieless asset origin with <code>Content-Type: image/svg+xml</code>, nosniff and CSP <code>default-src &#39;none&#39;; style-src &#39;none&#39;; sandbox</code>. The XML parser is pinned saxes 6.0.0 (upstream archived): monitor advisories/replacement; validation adds its own small whitelist and resource limits.</p>\n<h2 id=\"supply-chain-and-operations\">Supply chain and operations</h2>\n<p>No telemetry. Wrangler metrics are disabled in the local verification commands, but the external CLI has its own policy. Package files exclude local secrets, originals and credentials. Browser bundle scanning rejects Node/Sharp/filesystem/storage SDK leakage. Publication SDKs are optional build-only peers. Production containers are non-root, read-only, cap_drop ALL, no-new-privileges and bounded. Restrict /metrics and /purge at the CDN as defense in depth. Protect the Docker socket, CI provenance permissions and storage token scope. Keep Sharp/libvips and platform runtimes updated, and rerun the negative-input suite after upgrades. This repository&#39;s tests do not replace a third-party penetration test.</p>\n"
  },
  {
    "slug": "topology-and-costs",
    "title": "Platforms, topology and costs",
    "html": "<p>Reviewed 2026-10-19 from the official sources linked in each row. Prices are USD, before taxes and account-specific contracts. This is a research snapshot, not a price guarantee or a deployment test. Refresh pricing at each provider&#39;s implementation gate and before release.</p>\n<h2 id=\"topology\">Topology</h2>\n<p>Layer A delegates transformations to a configured image provider. Layer B is an image origin/service that should sit behind an actual CDN. Layer C publishes immutable build artifacts to storage with a public delivery base. These may be combined; the npm package includes no global distribution network, storage subscription, or third-party usage allowance.</p>\n<p>Recommended flows:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Managed: browser → provider CDN/optimizer → approved source origin.</li>\n<li>Self-hosted: browser → CDN → Imageway Node/Bun service → approved source origin.</li>\n<li>Worker: browser → Worker/cache → Images binding or transformation service → approved origin/R2.</li>\n<li>Static: browser → CDN/storage → prebuilt content-addressed artifact; no runtime transformation.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The service is not automatically an SSRF-safe network boundary merely because it is at the edge. A restricted object-key source is the preferred Worker mode.</p>\n<h2 id=\"main-providers\">Main providers</h2>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Platform</th>\n<th>Confirmed integration and restrictions</th>\n<th>Current cost signals</th>\n<th>Execution</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td><a href=\"https://developers.cloudflare.com/images/optimization/transformations/overview/\">Cloudflare remote transformations</a></td>\n<td><code>/cdn-cgi/image/</code> needs transformations enabled on the zone; origin restrictions apply. Worker <code>fetch</code> transformations and hosted Images are distinct modes</td>\n<td><a href=\"https://developers.cloudflare.com/images/pricing/\">Pricing</a>: first 5,000 unique transformations/month included; paid excess $0.50/1,000. Free blocks new transformations above allowance. Hosted storage $5/100,000 images/month and delivery $1/100,000 are separate meters</td>\n<td>Documentation only</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><a href=\"https://developers.cloudflare.com/images/optimization/binding/\">Cloudflare Images binding</a></td>\n<td>Stream input/transform/output; explicit output MIME required. Offline local mode supports fewer transforms than production</td>\n<td>Same transformation meter; Worker and storage costs can be additional</td>\n<td>Neither local nor remote mode run yet</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><a href=\"https://vercel.com/docs/image-optimization\">Vercel</a></td>\n<td>SvelteKit adapter/Build Output API must configure allowed sizes/origins/formats; <code>/_vercel/image</code> is platform infrastructure, not a general remote URL API</td>\n<td><a href=\"https://vercel.com/docs/image-optimization/limits-and-pricing\">Pricing</a>: Hobby includes 5K transforms, 300K read units, 100K write units/month. On-demand transforms $0.05–0.0812/1K; reads $0.40–0.64/1M; writes $4–6.40/1M. Cache units are 8KB. Delivery costs extra; Hobby limited to personal non-commercial use</td>\n<td>Documentation only</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><a href=\"https://docs.netlify.com/build/image-cdn/overview/\">Netlify</a></td>\n<td><code>/.netlify/images</code>; w/h, contain/cover/fill, format negotiation; remote images require explicit <code>remote_images</code>. Local-image invalidation follows atomic deploys</td>\n<td><a href=\"https://www.netlify.com/pricing/\">Pricing</a>: Free 300 credits/month; Personal $9/1,000 credits; Pro starts $20/3,000 credits. Delivery meters include 20 credits/GB and 2 credits/10K web requests. Do not promise unlimited free CDN traffic</td>\n<td>Documentation only</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><a href=\"https://bunny.net/pricing/optimizer/\">Bunny Optimizer</a></td>\n<td>Pull Zone add-on, not an included image origin; parameter/format/signature mapping requires M6 conformance checks</td>\n<td>$9.50/website/month. The <a href=\"https://bunny.net/faq/\">official FAQ</a> specifies per Pull Zone, billed hourly, with CDN bandwidth additional</td>\n<td>Documentation only</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><a href=\"https://cloudinary.com/pricing\">Cloudinary</a></td>\n<td>Managed assets and delivery transforms; signing and remote-fetch restrictions need explicit backend mapping</td>\n<td>Free plan 25 monthly credits. Credits combine resource meters; do not equate one credit with one transform or assume arbitrary remote fetch is enabled</td>\n<td>Pricing reviewed; API parity pending M6</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><a href=\"https://imagekit.io/plans/\">ImageKit</a></td>\n<td>URL endpoint/provider integration; transformation limits vary by plan</td>\n<td>Free: 20GB bandwidth/month, 3GB DAM storage. $9 plan: 40GB bandwidth, 10GB DAM; overages listed separately</td>\n<td>Pricing reviewed; API parity pending M6</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><a href=\"https://www.imgix.com/pricing\">imgix</a></td>\n<td>Managed source/provider; transformed media and delivery share credits</td>\n<td>Starts $25/month for 100 credits; 30-day trial. “Up to” storage/bandwidth figures are alternatives consuming a common allowance, not additive quotas</td>\n<td>Pricing reviewed; API parity pending M6</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><a href=\"https://supabase.com/docs/guides/storage/serving/image-transformations\">Supabase transformations</a></td>\n<td>Storage bucket images; resize enabled on Pro and above; SDK public/signed URL transforms</td>\n<td>Use the <a href=\"https://supabase.com/pricing\">account&#39;s current plan</a> and image/egress meters. Do not claim availability on Free or quote a per-image fee without its billing unit</td>\n<td>Documentation only</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><a href=\"https://www.fastly.com/products/image-optimizer\">Fastly Image Optimizer</a></td>\n<td>Edge resize, quality, crop, orientation and format conversion; service configuration required</td>\n<td><a href=\"https://www.fastly.com/pricing\">Pricing</a> is service/plan-specific; a final image workload quote is not established here</td>\n<td>Product reviewed; API limits and exact account cost pending M6</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/solutions/dynamic-image-transformation-for-amazon-cloudfront/\">CloudFront transformation solution</a></td>\n<td>CloudFront distribution plus deployed transformation infrastructure; not a built-in universal <code>?w=</code> optimizer</td>\n<td>CloudFront, processing, object storage, logs and requests can all contribute; use solution estimate and regional pricing</td>\n<td>Architecture reviewed; no stack deployed</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><a href=\"https://uploadcare.com/pricing/\">Uploadcare</a></td>\n<td>Optional managed-media provider candidate</td>\n<td>Current pricing page was reachable but a stable numerical quote was not exposed in the retrieved content. No numerical/free-tier claim is made</td>\n<td>Deferred optional provider until official API and billing evidence are captured</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><a href=\"https://docs.imgproxy.net/\">imgproxy</a> / <a href=\"https://github.com/unjs/ipx\">ipx</a></td>\n<td>Existing self-hosted backends, provider URLs map to their contracts; codecs/features depend on build and edition</td>\n<td>Software licensing/edition and compute/storage/CDN expenses are separate</td>\n<td>Documentation only</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>local / edge / generic</td>\n<td>local uses optional Sharp; edge uses v1; generic is a user-specified safe template</td>\n<td>Host compute, disk, network and CDN costs; no package usage fee</td>\n<td>Planned, no implementation</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<p>An API or cost marked pending is a research gap and a release gate, not an implemented capability. Especially do not market optional Uploadcare, Fastly parity, or all-provider feature parity on this evidence.</p>\n<h2 id=\"cloudflare-limits-affecting-design\">Cloudflare limits affecting design</h2>\n<p>The current <a href=\"https://developers.cloudflare.com/images/get-started/limits/\">Images limits</a> distinguish remote sources (100MB) from hosted uploads (10MB) and binding input (20MB). They also list 100MP image area limits, stricter animation handling, and an AVIF dimension limit of 1,200px. Model these as backend capabilities; do not promise all requested widths will produce AVIF. Local <code>wrangler dev</code> success would not establish production feature parity.</p>\n<p><a href=\"https://developers.cloudflare.com/workers/platform/limits/\">Workers limits</a>: 128MB per isolate, Free CPU 10ms/request and 100K requests/day, Paid CPU configurable up to five minutes with a lower default. Therefore a general-purpose AVIF WASM fallback is an experiment, not the default encoding plan. Stream bounded inputs; limit concurrent jobs. Cache API and <code>waitUntil</code> semantics must be tested independently. R2 is durable object storage, not a guarantee of globally warmed transformation caches.</p>\n<h2 id=\"storage-publication-targets\">Storage publication targets</h2>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Target</th>\n<th>Billing evidence and design consequence</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td><a href=\"https://developers.cloudflare.com/r2/pricing/\">R2 Standard</a></td>\n<td>10GB-month, 1M Class A and 10M Class B operations/month included; beyond: $0.015/GB-month, $4.50/M A and $0.36/M B. Direct R2 egress free; attached metered services can still charge. Use content hashes to avoid redundant writes</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><a href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/s3/pricing/\">S3 / CloudFront</a></td>\n<td>Region, storage class, requests and network affect price. No universal free-production-CDN claim; pricing calculation requires the selected region and account</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><a href=\"https://bunny.net/pricing/storage/\">Bunny Storage</a></td>\n<td>Replication/storage tier and CDN delivery are separate. Verify the selected regions before estimating uploads and retained variants</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><a href=\"https://www.backblaze.com/cloud-storage/pricing\">Backblaze B2</a></td>\n<td>S3-compatible target candidate; verify egress allowances and partner routes for the selected CDN</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><a href=\"https://vercel.com/docs/vercel-blob/usage-and-pricing\">Vercel Blob</a></td>\n<td>Storage, operations and delivery meters; not interchangeable with Vercel Image Optimization billing</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><a href=\"https://supabase.com/pricing\">Supabase Storage</a></td>\n<td>Storage/egress and transformations have different requirements; public bucket/CDN configuration must be explicit</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Generic S3 API</td>\n<td>Protocol compatibility does not imply identical ACLs, cache headers, checksum behavior, consistency, or pricing</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<p>A cost estimator should accept source count, permitted widths, crops, formats, bytes, requests, hit rate, refresh rate and retention. A bounded transform space constrains compute but does not prevent delivery, attack traffic or log charges. A distributed budget guard requires shared counters and provider/account limits; per-isolate counters are insufficient.</p>\n<h2 id=\"negotiated-cache-configuration\">Negotiated cache configuration</h2>\n<p>Explicit format URLs are the default and require the whole canonical image query in the CDN cache key. Never ignore source/width/format parameters.</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>CDN</th>\n<th>Negotiated mode policy</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td><a href=\"https://developers.cloudflare.com/cache/advanced-configuration/vary-for-images/\">Cloudflare</a></td>\n<td>Native Vary for Images is plan-gated (Pro/Business/Enterprise), configured for variants and extension-bearing paths. It does not automatically apply to an arbitrary <code>/_img</code> endpoint. For that endpoint, a Worker can negotiate into a canonical explicit-format internal cache URL</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Fastly</td>\n<td>Prefer explicit format. A normalized format key or a verified Vary configuration is required before enabling <code>auto</code>; exact service recipe remains a M5/M10 verification item</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Bunny</td>\n<td>Prefer explicit format or the optimizer&#39;s documented negotiation. Do not claim arbitrary origin Vary behavior; exact cache recipe remains a M6/M10 verification item</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonCloudFront/latest/DeveloperGuide/controlling-the-cache-key.html\">CloudFront</a></td>\n<td>Include required query fields in cache policy. For negotiated mode, normalize format at the edge into a bounded key; forwarding Accept to the origin without including the normalized variant in cache identity is unsafe</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<p>Raw <code>Accept</code>, DPR, Width and Save-Data must not multiply unbounded variants. The service can retain HTTP <code>Vary: Accept</code> for downstream correctness while internally storing by negotiated format. A mutable remote URL does not become immutable simply because the request is signed.</p>\n<h2 id=\"evidence-and-release-policy\">Evidence and release policy</h2>\n<p>No credentials were read, paid service activated, remote infrastructure created, or cloud deployment attempted. M4–M7 must capture real provider responses or label mock-only conformance tests. M10 must resolve the pending account-independent API/cache details and refresh numerical prices. Account-specific bills cannot be predicted without workload and subscription data.</p>\n"
  },
  {
    "slug": "adr-0002-url-contract",
    "title": "ADR 0002: Bounded v1 requests and explicit representation identity",
    "html": "<ul>\n<li>Date: 2026-10-19</li>\n<li>Status: Accepted for 0.1.0; parser/signature/policy-revision tests and machine schemas implemented</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"decision\">Decision</h2>\n<p>Use <code>/_img/v1</code> with a validated query contract. Exactly one of <code>url</code> and <code>key</code> identifies a source. Every provider accepts the same normalized request object and either maps the requested operations faithfully, invokes a documented degradation policy, or fails with an actionable capability error.</p>\n<p>Default to explicit output format URLs. Support <code>format=auto</code> only where the transport negotiates an allowed representation and includes that representation in its bounded cache identity. A <code>Vary</code> header alone does not configure a CDN cache policy.</p>\n<p>Normalization rejects duplicate/unknown parameters and invalid encodings, canonicalizes safe numerical/enum forms, applies configured width/quality ladders and serializes sorted parameters. Do not alter the meaning of signed upstream query strings. The source&#39;s authorization/tenant context, revision, pipeline version and representation are part of cache identity, though not all are public query fields.</p>\n<p>HMAC signs the version, endpoint, key ID, expiration and canonical request, excluding <code>sig</code>. Verification checks raw request validity, bounds, signature and expiration before serving any cached bytes. Expiration is not replaced by the transform cache&#39;s TTL. Rotation accepts explicitly configured current/previous keys; key IDs are public identifiers and secrets are server-only.</p>\n<p>For private output, default to private/no-store; a shared public CDN can otherwise bypass origin expiration checks on a hit. A public signed transformation is not necessarily private access control. Document the difference.</p>\n<h2 id=\"alternatives-rejected\">Alternatives rejected</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Raw query strings as cache keys: reordering/aliases and arbitrary widths explode variants.</li>\n<li>Signing an unnormalized request then normalizing separately: parser ambiguity creates signature and cache inconsistencies.</li>\n<li>Sorting or deleting arbitrary upstream source query parameters: can invalidate CMS/cloud signatures.</li>\n<li>Immutable caching for every signed URL: a valid signature proves authorization/integrity, not source immutability.</li>\n<li>Vendor-specific component props as the default abstraction: switching providers would require component rewrites.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"consequences\">Consequences</h2>\n<p>The <a href=\"/docs/url-contract\">draft specification</a> defines the planned vocabulary and algorithm. M1 must add JSON Schema/OpenAPI, canonical byte-level vectors, signature vectors, malformed query tests, preset expansion tests, expiry tests, rotation tests, and variant cardinality tests before changing this ADR to Accepted.</p>\n"
  },
  {
    "slug": "adr-0001-architecture",
    "title": "ADR 0001: One image model with separate execution boundaries",
    "html": "<ul>\n<li>Date: 2026-10-19</li>\n<li>Status: Proposed; implementation awaits M1 approval</li>\n<li>Scope: Svelte 5, current SvelteKit 3, Vite 8, managed providers, self-hosting, build publication</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"context\">Context</h2>\n<p>Local build optimization, remote CMS images and deployment-specific optimizers currently require different configuration and artifact shapes. A component should not need rewriting when an operator changes delivery infrastructure. The implementation must be honest about different backend capabilities, costs and runtime constraints.</p>\n<p>The user explicitly selected Bun. This supersedes the original brief&#39;s pnpm monorepo requirement. The brief also requires approval between milestones and a plan before implementation.</p>\n<h2 id=\"decision\">Decision</h2>\n<p>Use a Bun workspace with one public ESM/Svelte package and a private deployment workspace. All processing, delivery and publication paths share a versioned normalized image request and a serializable responsive artifact model. A provider may translate the request into a vendor-specific URL; the vendor&#39;s URL syntax need not be the v1 wire syntax. The Edge service itself accepts v1.</p>\n<p>Planned layout (not yet generated):</p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-text\">packages/svelte-imageway/    public package, src/lib subpath entry points\npackages/edge/              private Worker and Node service entry points\napps/docs/                  SvelteKit 3 documentation and playground\nexamples/basic/             first-image and hydration verification\nexamples/static/            prerender/build publication\nexamples/node/              runtime Sharp route\nexamples/cloudflare/        Worker integration\nexamples/cms/               remote allowlisted sources\nbench/                     fixture-driven reproducible comparisons\ndocs/                      ADRs, research, contract, release evidence\n</code></pre>\n<p>The public package exposes <code>.</code>, <code>./vite</code>, <code>./kit</code>, <code>./providers/*</code>, <code>./edge</code>, <code>./publish</code>, <code>./cli</code>, <code>./types</code>. Svelte compilation/declarations use <code>svelte-package</code>; worker/service entries must remain valid for their target runtime and be inspected in the packed artifact. Browser exports cannot re-export server barrels.</p>\n<h2 id=\"boundaries\">Boundaries</h2>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Portable core:</strong> pure normalization, directive parsing, srcset/sizes, provider contracts, public configuration, format negotiation. No Node imports, secrets, filesystem or Sharp.</li>\n<li><strong>Browser/Svelte:</strong> runes, snippets, SSR-safe rendering, native loading, dimensions and preload. Import only portable code. No runtime dependencies for basic rendering; measure generated bundle rather than source length.</li>\n<li><strong>Node tools/runtime:</strong> lazy optional Sharp import, workers, filesystem cache, secure source acquisition and upload targets. Signing keys resolve only from server environment. A missing Sharp installation yields an actionable error only when a local transformation is requested.</li>\n<li><strong>Worker edge:</strong> Web APIs plus supported platform bindings. Never bundle a Node Sharp binary. Prefer restricted storage keys and Images binding or <code>cf.image</code>; do not silently fall back to a WASM encoder with unbounded CPU.</li>\n<li><strong>Publication:</strong> transforms emit content-addressed artifacts, upload through <code>StorageTarget</code>, then rewrite only package-generated URLs. A validated <code>publicBase</code> must point to the uploaded artifacts. No automatic global <code>paths.assets</code> mutation.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Public and private configuration are separate values. A Vite virtual module may serialize only an explicit allowlist of public fields; it must not spread the user&#39;s config, functions, environment objects or secrets. Provider signing occurs server-side or at build time; client components never receive signing keys.</p>\n<h2 id=\"three-delivery-layers\">Three delivery layers</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>A: <code>ImageProvider</code> exposes pure <code>buildUrl</code>, capabilities and validation. Purge is optional and server-only. Vendor-specific server helpers live outside the portable provider entry if needed.</li>\n<li>B: the self-hosted service implements v1 and acts as an origin behind a real CDN. Node uses Sharp; Workers delegates to a supported binding or upstream service.</li>\n<li>C: publication uploads build variants incrementally with content hashes, resumes failures, writes a versioned manifest and supports dry-run. Garbage collection is a separate, conservative, explicitly approved action.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The generic provider can be approximately ten lines because shared validation and encoding are library helpers. Unsupported operations produce structured capability errors. A configurable documented degradation policy may drop optional operations; it cannot silently reinterpret cropping or reveal a private source.</p>\n<h2 id=\"defaults-and-performance\">Defaults and performance</h2>\n<p>Local-only operation needs no mandatory config. Proposed widths are a bounded ladder, clamped to intrinsic size, with no upscaling. Baseline outputs are AVIF/WebP plus a compatible fallback; production encoders and limits decide availability. LQIP (&lt;1KB target) is the initial placeholder because it displays without a browser decoder. Thumbhash/blurhash remain optional build/server strategies that may serialize a display-ready preview.</p>\n<p>Priority forces eager/high/sync attributes and head preload; it must not preload every candidate format and trigger duplicate downloads. Picture art direction needs media/type-aware preload matching. Unknown remote dimensions require supplied dimensions or a declared ratio; the component cannot synchronously infer them without source metadata. The CLS=0 claim applies to controlled examples, not arbitrary host CSS or missing dimension data.</p>\n<p>CSS provides a useful no-JavaScript placeholder. Minimal component state handles loaded/cached/error transitions, user callbacks and reduced motion. Shared configuration uses a typed Svelte context per subtree; never a mutable module-global request configuration during SSR.</p>\n<h2 id=\"security-from-the-first-service-code\">Security from the first service code</h2>\n<p>Remote fetches require explicit HTTPS origins, restricted ports and bounded redirects. Node validates all resolved A/AAAA addresses, blocks local/private/link-local/metadata/mapped encodings, and pins the validated connection address while preserving the approved hostname for Host/TLS. Revalidate every redirect. If a runtime cannot safely enforce DNS/connection policy, disable arbitrary remote fetching and require trusted storage keys or an independently secured upstream.</p>\n<p>The threat assessment follows the <a href=\"https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Server_Side_Request_Forgery_Prevention_Cheat_Sheet.html\">OWASP SSRF prevention guidance</a>; the specific runtime/transport controls still require adversarial execution tests.</p>\n<p>Local sources use realpath containment inside approved roots, including symlink escapes. Enforce bytes, decoded pixels/pages, output bytes, timeouts, queue/concurrency limits and magic-byte checks. Reject active SVG or rasterize in a restricted pipeline; SVGO is not a sanitizer. Disable unsafe decoder settings. Strip sensitive metadata by default.</p>\n<p>HMAC-SHA256 signing, key IDs, expiry, rotation, constant-time verification, locked presets and canonicalization are specified in ADR 0002. Authentication and expiration checks occur before cache reads. Public caching must not expose private images or outlive a private authorization contract.</p>\n<h2 id=\"cache-and-reliability\">Cache and reliability</h2>\n<p>Build cache identity contains input bytes, normalized transformations, pipeline/codec version and relevant output policy. Write atomically and deduplicate jobs. Runtime cache adds source identity/revision and output MIME. Mutable sources require TTL/revalidation; signatures alone never justify immutable caching.</p>\n<p>Memory LRU is bounded by bytes, disk by configured storage budget; in-flight maps are bounded. Concurrent deduplication is per process/isolate unless a shared backend is configured. Structured logging excludes client IPs and secret-bearing source query strings by default.</p>\n<p>Fallback/circuit breaking belongs in the runtime transport, since a pure URL builder cannot observe HTTP 5xx or enforce a timeout. Browser error callbacks cannot reliably distinguish status codes. Server fallback stops on authorization/validation failures, preserves policy, applies deadlines and retries only eligible errors. Returning the original source is allowed only for public sources explicitly configured for fallback.</p>\n<h2 id=\"adapter-strategy\">Adapter strategy</h2>\n<p><code>imageAdapter()</code> wraps a supplied current SvelteKit adapter, preserving its interface. Detect recognized adapters using documented metadata/capabilities where available; provide an explicit target override for auto/unknown adapters. Never rely on names of nonexistent packages or guess an edge runtime from a hostname.</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Target</th>\n<th>Proposed behavior</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td>static</td>\n<td>Build variants and optional publication; no dynamic Sharp endpoint</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Node / verified Bun</td>\n<td>Sharp runtime with bounded memory/disk cache</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Vercel</td>\n<td>Emit supported platform image configuration; delegate to native image service</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Netlify</td>\n<td>Use Image CDN and validated remote patterns</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Cloudflare</td>\n<td>Binding/transformation provider; no native Sharp</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>auto / unknown / Deno</td>\n<td>Detect supported configuration or require an explicit target; actionable failure for impossible combinations</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<p>Ordinary Link headers and head preload are portable targets. SvelteKit streaming cannot guarantee discovery before headers flush; track preload hints per request or declare them earlier. 103 Early Hints remain platform-dependent and require specific evidence. Do not promise to add them through a generic Fetch Response.</p>\n<h2 id=\"dependency-decisions\">Dependency decisions</h2>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Dependency</th>\n<th>Purpose and justification</th>\n<th>Placement</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td>Svelte</td>\n<td>Required component peer, ^5 baseline</td>\n<td>Peer</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>SvelteKit/Vite/Svelte plugin</td>\n<td>Current integration contracts; examples and build tooling</td>\n<td>Optional integration peers/dev</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>@sveltejs/package / TypeScript 6</td>\n<td>Supported package output and strict declarations; TS7 peer conflict documented</td>\n<td>Development</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Sharp</td>\n<td>Existing high-quality codec/pixel pipeline; no homegrown codecs</td>\n<td>Optional server/build peer, dev fixture dependency</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>SVGO</td>\n<td>Optimizes approved SVG; cannot supply security guarantees</td>\n<td>Server/build only, when introduced</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>thumbhash / blurhash</td>\n<td>Optional placeholder algorithms if they meet no-JS and bundle requirements</td>\n<td>Server/build optional</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Vitest / coverage / Playwright</td>\n<td>Unit/type/SSR/browser/security regression evidence</td>\n<td>Development; versions aligned</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Wrangler / Worker types</td>\n<td>Actual local Worker runtime and bindings</td>\n<td>Edge development only</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>AWS S3 SDK</td>\n<td>Signing, retries and interoperability justify a scoped SDK when S3 targets are built</td>\n<td>Optional publish-only dependency in M7</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>ESLint / Svelte plugin / Prettier</td>\n<td>Framework-aware lint and formatting</td>\n<td>Development</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Changesets / commitlint / Husky / lint-staged</td>\n<td>Release history, commit conventions and local changed-file checks</td>\n<td>Development; CI remains authoritative</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>OpenTelemetry API</td>\n<td>Optional observability hooks without mandatory exporters</td>\n<td>Optional server integration only</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<p>Use native URL, Web Crypto, fetch, filesystem primitives and small explicit validation for the narrow contract. Generate machine schemas and check them against the validator; do not add a large runtime schema framework by default. If security requires a well-maintained IP parser or sanitization library, record its evidence and justification before addition. Do not implement a fragile ad-hoc IP regular expression.</p>\n<h2 id=\"alternatives-rejected\">Alternatives rejected</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>A bundled “global CDN”: not something an npm package can provide.</li>\n<li>Mandatory Sharp in portable/browser exports: breaks edge isolation and inflates installs.</li>\n<li>A wrapper around enhanced-img plus an unrelated proxy: easier initially but leaves two contracts/configuration systems.</li>\n<li>Full feature parity across every provider: false for formats, smart cropping, animation, purge and signing.</li>\n<li>Automatically adding a global hook to an arbitrary app: fragile and may break existing hooks; offer an explicit composable handle and narrowly scoped validated integration.</li>\n<li>WASM as the universal Worker default: CPU, memory, binary size and codec support need measurements.</li>\n<li>Automatic paid deployment/publication: credentials, bills and destructive operations need an explicit reviewed action.</li>\n<li>pnpm workspace: superseded by the user&#39;s Bun instruction.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"consequences-and-gates\">Consequences and gates</h2>\n<p>This is a substantial multi-milestone project. Phase 0 establishes a design, not runtime behavior. M1 must prove the pure/Node boundary and &gt;90% core coverage; M2 proves actual Vite emitted assets/dev HMR; M3 proves SSR/hydration/bundle size; later gates prove each deployment path. Keep every claim&#39;s evidence in REPORT.md and stop after each milestone for the user&#39;s approval.</p>\n"
  },
  {
    "slug": "research-versions-and-compatibility",
    "title": "Versions and compatibility",
    "html": "<p>Checked 2026-10-19. The registry&#39;s <code>latest</code> tag determines the current stable package selection; beta, next, RC, and development tags are not selected. Metadata compatibility is not execution compatibility.</p>\n<h2 id=\"proposed-baseline\">Proposed baseline</h2>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Dependency</th>\n<th>Stable observed</th>\n<th>Selection</th>\n<th>Reason</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td>Svelte</td>\n<td>5.57.2</td>\n<td>5.57.2</td>\n<td>Svelte 5 only; meets Kit&#39;s ^5.57.1 peer</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>SvelteKit</td>\n<td>3.1.0</td>\n<td>3.1.0</td>\n<td>Current stable; use Kit 3 integration and configuration</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Vite</td>\n<td>8.3.4</td>\n<td>8.3.4</td>\n<td>Satisfies Kit ^8.0.12 and Svelte plugin ^8</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>@sveltejs/vite-plugin-svelte</td>\n<td>7.3.1</td>\n<td>7.3.1</td>\n<td>Satisfies Kit ^7</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>@sveltejs/package</td>\n<td>3.0.1</td>\n<td>3.0.1</td>\n<td>Supported package compilation/declarations; TypeScript ^6 peer</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>sharp</td>\n<td>0.35.5</td>\n<td>0.35.5</td>\n<td>Node &gt;=20.9; optional server dependency</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Vitest</td>\n<td>5.0.5</td>\n<td>5.0.5</td>\n<td>Supports Vite 8; matching coverage/browser packages required</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>@playwright/test</td>\n<td>1.64.1</td>\n<td>1.64.1</td>\n<td>Stable browser test runner; browser binaries not yet installed</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Wrangler</td>\n<td>4.151.2</td>\n<td>4.151.2</td>\n<td>Node &gt;=22; current Worker tooling</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>TypeScript</td>\n<td>7.0.2</td>\n<td><strong>6.0.3</strong></td>\n<td>Latest stable 7 is outside Kit/package ^6 peer ranges; latest compatible 6 selected</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>SVGO</td>\n<td>4.1.0</td>\n<td>4.1.0 when needed</td>\n<td>SVG optimization; not a security sanitizer</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>thumbhash</td>\n<td>0.1.1</td>\n<td>Optional, evaluated in M2</td>\n<td>Build/server generation only; avoid mandatory browser decoder</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>blurhash</td>\n<td>2.0.5</td>\n<td>Optional, evaluated in M2</td>\n<td>User-requested alternative; same client budget constraint</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Bun</td>\n<td>Installed 1.4.3</td>\n<td>1.4.3 workspace tool</td>\n<td>Required by user; runtime support must be tested independently</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<p>Primary evidence: <a href=\"/references/docs/research/evidence/npm-view-2026-10-19.json\">npm responses</a>. TypeScript 6.0.3 was additionally checked with <code>npm view typescript@6.0.3 version engines --json</code> (Node &gt;=14.17).</p>\n<h2 id=\"node-lts\">Node LTS</h2>\n<p>The <a href=\"https://nodejs.org/dist/index.json\">official release index</a> returned Node <strong>24.22.0</strong> (Krypton LTS, 2026-10-14), <strong>22.23.3</strong> (Jod LTS, 2026-09-23), and 26.12.0 (Current, 2026-10-14). The <a href=\"https://nodejs.org/en/about/previous-releases\">release policy</a> and <a href=\"https://github.com/nodejs/Release\">release schedule</a> distinguish Current from LTS. Node 26 must not be described as LTS on this research date.</p>\n<p>Choose Node 24.22.0 for development/CI baseline and 22.23.3 for the second LTS lane. A future Node 26 LTS lane can be added after the official transition and verification. Proposed minimum for the Kit integration is Node 22.17, with CI on maintained LTS patches. Do not infer Windows/Linux support from this Mac.</p>\n<p>Observed host: Node 26.11.1, npm 11.20.0, Bun 1.4.3. This host&#39;s Node is Current and is not the baseline LTS. M1 must arrange an isolated Node 24 runtime without changing the user&#39;s global installation.</p>\n<h2 id=\"adapter-versions\">Adapter versions</h2>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Official adapter</th>\n<th>Stable observed</th>\n<th>Declared Kit peer</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td>static</td>\n<td>4.0.0</td>\n<td>^3.0.0-next.0</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>node</td>\n<td>6.0.0</td>\n<td>^3.0.0-next.0</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>auto</td>\n<td>8.0.0</td>\n<td>^3.0.0-next.0</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>cloudflare</td>\n<td>8.0.1</td>\n<td>^3.0.0-next.0; Wrangler ^4.118.0</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>vercel</td>\n<td>7.0.1</td>\n<td>^3.0.0-next.0</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>netlify</td>\n<td>7.0.1</td>\n<td>^3.0.0-next.0</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<p>Bun and Deno adapters are not assigned invented package names or compatibility claims. Follow the <a href=\"https://svelte.dev/docs/kit/adapters\">official adapter index</a> and inspect actual adapter packages before implementation.</p>\n<h2 id=\"integration-changes-that-affect-design\">Integration changes that affect design</h2>\n<p>The <a href=\"https://svelte.dev/docs/kit/migrating-to-sveltekit-3\">Kit 3 migration guide</a> moves configuration into the <code>sveltekit()</code> Vite plugin; old <code>svelte.config.js</code> examples are unsuitable for a new Kit 3 application. <code>imageAdapter()</code> must work with the current adapter interface rather than inspect a legacy config file.</p>\n<p><a href=\"https://svelte.dev/docs/kit/adapter-vercel\">Vercel&#39;s Kit adapter</a> exposes image configuration. Use the <a href=\"https://vercel.com/docs/build-output-api/v3/configuration#images\">Build Output API</a> rather than pretending <code>/_vercel/image</code> works on arbitrary hosting.</p>\n<p>The publication plugin should rewrite the URLs of its own emitted artifacts through Vite asset references and a validated public base. Changing global <code>paths.assets</code> is not required and could accidentally move unrelated app assets; confirm the final mechanism against <a href=\"https://svelte.dev/docs/kit/@sveltejs-kit-vite\">Kit configuration</a> and Vite&#39;s emitted-asset behavior in M2/M7.</p>\n<p>For <code>sizes=&quot;auto&quot;</code>, <a href=\"https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/HTML/Reference/Elements/img#sizes\">MDN&#39;s current element reference</a> restricts it to lazy images and recommends fallback sizes. The default remains explicit sizes until a current browser-compatibility matrix is captured in M3. Offer <code>auto, 100vw</code> as a progressive option for lazy images only. Never use auto sizing for an eager priority preload.</p>\n<h2 id=\"execution-status\">Execution status</h2>\n<p>Registry requests, official documentation reads, host version checks and power assertions were executed. Dependency installation, Kit compatibility builds, Sharp codec probes, tests, Wrangler, browser tests, Docker, packaging and benchmarks have <strong>not</strong> been executed. Docker is absent from PATH. No pass is inferred from version metadata.</p>\n"
  },
  {
    "slug": "research-provider-contracts",
    "title": "Provider contract verification — 2026-10-20",
    "html": "<p>URL conformance tests are local, deterministic tests, not proof of paid-account activation. A real public Cloudinary demo transform was additionally fetched and decoded (see validation evidence). Capabilities deliberately expose a conservative intersection; unsupported operations throw 422 instead of disappearing. The portable v1 request is translated to each native API, not appended blindly.</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Provider</th>\n<th>Implemented contract</th>\n<th>Official reference / constraint</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td>Bunny</td>\n<td>inside resize, width/height/quality/format</td>\n<td><a href=\"https://docs.bunny.net/docs/stream-image-processing/\">Dynamic Images</a>; enable Optimizer on the Pull Zone; no AVIF declaration</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>imgix</td>\n<td>max/crop/scale/fill, q/fm/auto</td>\n<td><a href=\"https://docs.imgix.com/en-US/apis/rendering/size/resize-fit-mode\">Resize fit</a>; signed URL mutation rejected</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Cloudinary</td>\n<td>upload public IDs or fetch, c_limit/fill/pad/scale, w/h/q/f</td>\n<td><a href=\"https://cloudinary.com/documentation/transformation_reference\">Transformation reference</a>; fetch delivery may need enabling, secure account with strict transformations</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>ImageKit</td>\n<td>c-at_max, w/h/q/f</td>\n<td><a href=\"https://imagekit.io/docs/image-resize-and-crop\">Resize/crop</a>; signed URLs must be regenerated on the server</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>imgproxy</td>\n<td>rs:fit/fill, q, escaped plain source</td>\n<td><a href=\"https://docs.imgproxy.net/usage/processing\">Processing</a>; unsafe mode requires explicit opt-in and a private protected origin, never an open public optimizer</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>IPX</td>\n<td>w/h/q/f modifiers</td>\n<td><a href=\"https://github.com/unjs/ipx\">Official project</a>; configure its storage allowlist independently</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Supabase</td>\n<td>public render endpoint, width/height/quality/resize</td>\n<td><a href=\"https://supabase.com/docs/guides/storage/serving/image-transformations\">Transformations</a>; format is negotiated, signed tokens are generated server-side, plan restrictions apply</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Fastly</td>\n<td>bounds, width/height/quality/format</td>\n<td><a href=\"https://www.fastly.com/documentation/reference/io/\">IO reference</a>; product entitlement required; may fall back at native format limits, therefore no typed explicit-format sources</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>CloudFront</td>\n<td>Imageway v1 origin behind distribution</td>\n<td><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonCloudFront/latest/DeveloperGuide/QueryStringParameters.html\">Query forwarding/cache</a>; CloudFront itself is not an encoder; this does not impersonate the separate AWS image solution</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Uploadcare</td>\n<td>preview, quality presets, format</td>\n<td><a href=\"https://uploadcare.com/docs/transformations/image/resize-crop/\">Resize</a>, <a href=\"https://uploadcare.com/docs/compression/\">quality</a>; numeric quality is mapped to native named presets</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<p>CMS: <a href=\"https://www.sanity.io/docs/apis-and-sdks/presenting-images\">Sanity</a>, <a href=\"https://www.contentful.com/developers/docs/references/images-api/images/\">Contentful</a>, <a href=\"https://www.storyblok.com/docs/api/image-service\">Storyblok</a>, <a href=\"https://docs.directus.io/reference/files\">Directus</a>, <a href=\"https://shopify.dev/docs/api/liquid/filters/image_url\">Shopify</a>, <a href=\"https://prismic.io/docs/fields/image\">Prismic</a>, <a href=\"https://www.datocms.com/docs/content-delivery-api/images-and-videos\">DatoCMS</a>, <a href=\"https://unsplash.com/documentation\">Unsplash</a>. Unsplash attribution query is retained; consumers must still satisfy API attribution/hotlink obligations. Self-hosted Directus needs an explicit kind/base. Strapi and WordPress have no universal resize query: map their media response variants to <code>cmsArtifact</code>. See <a href=\"https://docs.strapi.io/cms/features/media-library\">Strapi media</a> and <a href=\"https://developer.wordpress.org/rest-api/reference/media/\">WordPress media</a>.</p>\n<p>All URL builders are credential-free. No platform purge API is implied by a URL provider. Use the protected Imageway Worker purge API or the platform SDK in a server-only module. Signed third-party transforms require vendor server signing; universal Imageway HMAC is not interchangeable with vendor signatures.</p>\n<p><code>createFallback</code> is server transport orchestration, not a browser retry loop. Its required transport must enforce the source policy. It retries network/timeouts/5xx, opens a per-instance circuit, uses a half-open probe and stops on 4xx (including authentication and budget errors). Original fallback is an explicit authorizing callback. Global circuit state requires an operator-supplied shared service; no private image becomes public on failure.</p>\n"
  },
  {
    "slug": "research-platforms-and-costs",
    "title": "Platforms, topology and costs",
    "html": "<p>Reviewed 2026-10-19 from the official sources linked in each row. Prices are USD, before taxes and account-specific contracts. This is a research snapshot, not a price guarantee or a deployment test. Refresh pricing at each provider&#39;s implementation gate and before release.</p>\n<h2 id=\"topology\">Topology</h2>\n<p>Layer A delegates transformations to a configured image provider. Layer B is an image origin/service that should sit behind an actual CDN. Layer C publishes immutable build artifacts to storage with a public delivery base. These may be combined; the npm package includes no global distribution network, storage subscription, or third-party usage allowance.</p>\n<p>Recommended flows:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Managed: browser → provider CDN/optimizer → approved source origin.</li>\n<li>Self-hosted: browser → CDN → Imageway Node/Bun service → approved source origin.</li>\n<li>Worker: browser → Worker/cache → Images binding or transformation service → approved origin/R2.</li>\n<li>Static: browser → CDN/storage → prebuilt content-addressed artifact; no runtime transformation.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The service is not automatically an SSRF-safe network boundary merely because it is at the edge. A restricted object-key source is the preferred Worker mode.</p>\n<h2 id=\"main-providers\">Main providers</h2>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Platform</th>\n<th>Confirmed integration and restrictions</th>\n<th>Current cost signals</th>\n<th>Execution</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td><a href=\"https://developers.cloudflare.com/images/optimization/transformations/overview/\">Cloudflare remote transformations</a></td>\n<td><code>/cdn-cgi/image/</code> needs transformations enabled on the zone; origin restrictions apply. Worker <code>fetch</code> transformations and hosted Images are distinct modes</td>\n<td><a href=\"https://developers.cloudflare.com/images/pricing/\">Pricing</a>: first 5,000 unique transformations/month included; paid excess $0.50/1,000. Free blocks new transformations above allowance. Hosted storage $5/100,000 images/month and delivery $1/100,000 are separate meters</td>\n<td>Documentation only</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><a href=\"https://developers.cloudflare.com/images/optimization/binding/\">Cloudflare Images binding</a></td>\n<td>Stream input/transform/output; explicit output MIME required. Offline local mode supports fewer transforms than production</td>\n<td>Same transformation meter; Worker and storage costs can be additional</td>\n<td>Neither local nor remote mode run yet</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><a href=\"https://vercel.com/docs/image-optimization\">Vercel</a></td>\n<td>SvelteKit adapter/Build Output API must configure allowed sizes/origins/formats; <code>/_vercel/image</code> is platform infrastructure, not a general remote URL API</td>\n<td><a href=\"https://vercel.com/docs/image-optimization/limits-and-pricing\">Pricing</a>: Hobby includes 5K transforms, 300K read units, 100K write units/month. On-demand transforms $0.05–0.0812/1K; reads $0.40–0.64/1M; writes $4–6.40/1M. Cache units are 8KB. Delivery costs extra; Hobby limited to personal non-commercial use</td>\n<td>Documentation only</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><a href=\"https://docs.netlify.com/build/image-cdn/overview/\">Netlify</a></td>\n<td><code>/.netlify/images</code>; w/h, contain/cover/fill, format negotiation; remote images require explicit <code>remote_images</code>. Local-image invalidation follows atomic deploys</td>\n<td><a href=\"https://www.netlify.com/pricing/\">Pricing</a>: Free 300 credits/month; Personal $9/1,000 credits; Pro starts $20/3,000 credits. Delivery meters include 20 credits/GB and 2 credits/10K web requests. Do not promise unlimited free CDN traffic</td>\n<td>Documentation only</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><a href=\"https://bunny.net/pricing/optimizer/\">Bunny Optimizer</a></td>\n<td>Pull Zone add-on, not an included image origin; parameter/format/signature mapping requires M6 conformance checks</td>\n<td>$9.50/website/month. The <a href=\"https://bunny.net/faq/\">official FAQ</a> specifies per Pull Zone, billed hourly, with CDN bandwidth additional</td>\n<td>Documentation only</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><a href=\"https://cloudinary.com/pricing\">Cloudinary</a></td>\n<td>Managed assets and delivery transforms; signing and remote-fetch restrictions need explicit backend mapping</td>\n<td>Free plan 25 monthly credits. Credits combine resource meters; do not equate one credit with one transform or assume arbitrary remote fetch is enabled</td>\n<td>Pricing reviewed; API parity pending M6</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><a href=\"https://imagekit.io/plans/\">ImageKit</a></td>\n<td>URL endpoint/provider integration; transformation limits vary by plan</td>\n<td>Free: 20GB bandwidth/month, 3GB DAM storage. $9 plan: 40GB bandwidth, 10GB DAM; overages listed separately</td>\n<td>Pricing reviewed; API parity pending M6</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><a href=\"https://www.imgix.com/pricing\">imgix</a></td>\n<td>Managed source/provider; transformed media and delivery share credits</td>\n<td>Starts $25/month for 100 credits; 30-day trial. “Up to” storage/bandwidth figures are alternatives consuming a common allowance, not additive quotas</td>\n<td>Pricing reviewed; API parity pending M6</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><a href=\"https://supabase.com/docs/guides/storage/serving/image-transformations\">Supabase transformations</a></td>\n<td>Storage bucket images; resize enabled on Pro and above; SDK public/signed URL transforms</td>\n<td>Use the <a href=\"https://supabase.com/pricing\">account&#39;s current plan</a> and image/egress meters. Do not claim availability on Free or quote a per-image fee without its billing unit</td>\n<td>Documentation only</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><a href=\"https://www.fastly.com/products/image-optimizer\">Fastly Image Optimizer</a></td>\n<td>Edge resize, quality, crop, orientation and format conversion; service configuration required</td>\n<td><a href=\"https://www.fastly.com/pricing\">Pricing</a> is service/plan-specific; a final image workload quote is not established here</td>\n<td>Product reviewed; API limits and exact account cost pending M6</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/solutions/dynamic-image-transformation-for-amazon-cloudfront/\">CloudFront transformation solution</a></td>\n<td>CloudFront distribution plus deployed transformation infrastructure; not a built-in universal <code>?w=</code> optimizer</td>\n<td>CloudFront, processing, object storage, logs and requests can all contribute; use solution estimate and regional pricing</td>\n<td>Architecture reviewed; no stack deployed</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><a href=\"https://uploadcare.com/pricing/\">Uploadcare</a></td>\n<td>Optional managed-media provider candidate</td>\n<td>Current pricing page was reachable but a stable numerical quote was not exposed in the retrieved content. No numerical/free-tier claim is made</td>\n<td>Deferred optional provider until official API and billing evidence are captured</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><a href=\"https://docs.imgproxy.net/\">imgproxy</a> / <a href=\"https://github.com/unjs/ipx\">ipx</a></td>\n<td>Existing self-hosted backends, provider URLs map to their contracts; codecs/features depend on build and edition</td>\n<td>Software licensing/edition and compute/storage/CDN expenses are separate</td>\n<td>Documentation only</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>local / edge / generic</td>\n<td>local uses optional Sharp; edge uses v1; generic is a user-specified safe template</td>\n<td>Host compute, disk, network and CDN costs; no package usage fee</td>\n<td>Planned, no implementation</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<p>An API or cost marked pending is a research gap and a release gate, not an implemented capability. Especially do not market optional Uploadcare, Fastly parity, or all-provider feature parity on this evidence.</p>\n<h2 id=\"cloudflare-limits-affecting-design\">Cloudflare limits affecting design</h2>\n<p>The current <a href=\"https://developers.cloudflare.com/images/get-started/limits/\">Images limits</a> distinguish remote sources (100MB) from hosted uploads (10MB) and binding input (20MB). They also list 100MP image area limits, stricter animation handling, and an AVIF dimension limit of 1,200px. Model these as backend capabilities; do not promise all requested widths will produce AVIF. Local <code>wrangler dev</code> success would not establish production feature parity.</p>\n<p><a href=\"https://developers.cloudflare.com/workers/platform/limits/\">Workers limits</a>: 128MB per isolate, Free CPU 10ms/request and 100K requests/day, Paid CPU configurable up to five minutes with a lower default. Therefore a general-purpose AVIF WASM fallback is an experiment, not the default encoding plan. Stream bounded inputs; limit concurrent jobs. Cache API and <code>waitUntil</code> semantics must be tested independently. R2 is durable object storage, not a guarantee of globally warmed transformation caches.</p>\n<h2 id=\"storage-publication-targets\">Storage publication targets</h2>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Target</th>\n<th>Billing evidence and design consequence</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td><a href=\"https://developers.cloudflare.com/r2/pricing/\">R2 Standard</a></td>\n<td>10GB-month, 1M Class A and 10M Class B operations/month included; beyond: $0.015/GB-month, $4.50/M A and $0.36/M B. Direct R2 egress free; attached metered services can still charge. Use content hashes to avoid redundant writes</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><a href=\"https://aws.amazon.com/s3/pricing/\">S3 / CloudFront</a></td>\n<td>Region, storage class, requests and network affect price. No universal free-production-CDN claim; pricing calculation requires the selected region and account</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><a href=\"https://bunny.net/pricing/storage/\">Bunny Storage</a></td>\n<td>Replication/storage tier and CDN delivery are separate. Verify the selected regions before estimating uploads and retained variants</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><a href=\"https://www.backblaze.com/cloud-storage/pricing\">Backblaze B2</a></td>\n<td>S3-compatible target candidate; verify egress allowances and partner routes for the selected CDN</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><a href=\"https://vercel.com/docs/vercel-blob/usage-and-pricing\">Vercel Blob</a></td>\n<td>Storage, operations and delivery meters; not interchangeable with Vercel Image Optimization billing</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><a href=\"https://supabase.com/pricing\">Supabase Storage</a></td>\n<td>Storage/egress and transformations have different requirements; public bucket/CDN configuration must be explicit</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Generic S3 API</td>\n<td>Protocol compatibility does not imply identical ACLs, cache headers, checksum behavior, consistency, or pricing</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<p>A cost estimator should accept source count, permitted widths, crops, formats, bytes, requests, hit rate, refresh rate and retention. A bounded transform space constrains compute but does not prevent delivery, attack traffic or log charges. A distributed budget guard requires shared counters and provider/account limits; per-isolate counters are insufficient.</p>\n<h2 id=\"negotiated-cache-configuration\">Negotiated cache configuration</h2>\n<p>Explicit format URLs are the default and require the whole canonical image query in the CDN cache key. Never ignore source/width/format parameters.</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>CDN</th>\n<th>Negotiated mode policy</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td><a href=\"https://developers.cloudflare.com/cache/advanced-configuration/vary-for-images/\">Cloudflare</a></td>\n<td>Native Vary for Images is plan-gated (Pro/Business/Enterprise), configured for variants and extension-bearing paths. It does not automatically apply to an arbitrary <code>/_img</code> endpoint. For that endpoint, a Worker can negotiate into a canonical explicit-format internal cache URL</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Fastly</td>\n<td>Prefer explicit format. A normalized format key or a verified Vary configuration is required before enabling <code>auto</code>; exact service recipe remains a M5/M10 verification item</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Bunny</td>\n<td>Prefer explicit format or the optimizer&#39;s documented negotiation. Do not claim arbitrary origin Vary behavior; exact cache recipe remains a M6/M10 verification item</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><a href=\"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/AmazonCloudFront/latest/DeveloperGuide/controlling-the-cache-key.html\">CloudFront</a></td>\n<td>Include required query fields in cache policy. For negotiated mode, normalize format at the edge into a bounded key; forwarding Accept to the origin without including the normalized variant in cache identity is unsafe</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<p>Raw <code>Accept</code>, DPR, Width and Save-Data must not multiply unbounded variants. The service can retain HTTP <code>Vary: Accept</code> for downstream correctness while internally storing by negotiated format. A mutable remote URL does not become immutable simply because the request is signed.</p>\n<h2 id=\"evidence-and-release-policy\">Evidence and release policy</h2>\n<p>No credentials were read, paid service activated, remote infrastructure created, or cloud deployment attempted. M4–M7 must capture real provider responses or label mock-only conformance tests. M10 must resolve the pending account-independent API/cache details and refresh numerical prices. Account-specific bills cannot be predicted without workload and subscription data.</p>\n"
  },
  {
    "slug": "research-competitive-analysis",
    "title": "Competitive analysis",
    "html": "<p>Research date: 2026-10-19. Versions and registry modification dates below come from <a href=\"/references/docs/research/evidence/npm-view-2026-10-19.json\">npm view evidence</a>. A registry modification date is not necessarily a release or last source commit. Maintenance observations are signals, not guarantees of responsiveness.</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Project</th>\n<th>Capabilities and DX</th>\n<th>Limits relevant to this mission</th>\n<th>Maintenance evidence</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td><a href=\"https://svelte.dev/docs/kit/images\">@sveltejs/enhanced-img</a> 1.0.0</td>\n<td>Official Vite preprocessing; local responsive formats, dimensions, metadata removal and persistent build cache; <code>&lt;enhanced:img&gt;</code> integration</td>\n<td>Requires build-accessible files; does not unify remote service operation and object-store publication</td>\n<td>Official SvelteKit project; registry modified 2026-10-01; current registry supersedes stale search snippets describing 0.x</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><a href=\"https://github.com/JonasKruckenberg/imagetools\">vite-imagetools</a> 12.0.1</td>\n<td>Sharp-based import directives, format conversion, resizing, srcsets, extensible transforms</td>\n<td>Build tooling rather than a Svelte component family or a deployable managed runtime</td>\n<td>Registry modified 2026-08-28; current package declares Vite &gt;=8</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><a href=\"https://github.com/metonym/svelte-img\">svelte-img</a> 0.4.0</td>\n<td>Responsive image presentation, loading transition, documented no-JavaScript rendering</td>\n<td>Does not provide the requested local transformer, universal provider contract, or service; Svelte 5 runes support is unverified</td>\n<td>Registry modified 2022-05-19; age warrants a compatibility test, not an unsupported “abandoned” claim</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><a href=\"https://unpic.pics/img/svelte/\">@unpic/svelte</a> 1.0.1</td>\n<td>Strong existing CDN/CMS recognition, responsive layout, priority, native attributes and configurable operations</td>\n<td>A serious overlapping competitor; provider operations can differ; does not by itself supply this project&#39;s build/upload/service lifecycle</td>\n<td>Peer dependency Svelte ^5; registry modified 2025-12-08</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><a href=\"https://github.com/unjs/ipx\">ipx</a> 4.0.0</td>\n<td>Standalone server optimizer, Sharp-backed transforms, local/HTTP sources, URL-based requests</td>\n<td>Good server primitive; not the full Svelte 5 authoring/build/provider/publication experience</td>\n<td>Registry modified 2026-10-15; Node ^20.16 or &gt;=22.3</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><a href=\"https://github.com/humanshield-sidepack/sveltekit-image-optimize\">sveltekit-image-optimize</a> 0.0.10</td>\n<td>SvelteKit hook, remote transforms, Image/Picture, memory/filesystem/S3 cache adapters</td>\n<td>Overlaps runtime and components substantially; Sharp peer stops at ^0.34 in current metadata; Kit 3 compatibility unverified</td>\n<td>Svelte ^5 peer; registry modified 2025-06-14; early versioning</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><a href=\"https://docs.imgproxy.net/\">imgproxy</a></td>\n<td>Established self-hosted image server and signing model; useful backend alternative</td>\n<td>External service rather than Svelte build integration; edition-specific capabilities must be declared</td>\n<td>Official documentation reviewed; no unsupported inference about release cadence</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<h2 id=\"value-proposition\">Value proposition</h2>\n<p>Imageway&#39;s proposed value is lifecycle consistency: one bounded, versioned image request model drives build optimization, dynamic remote optimization, Svelte 5 presentation, CLI audit/optimization/deployment, adapter selection, and CDN/storage integrations. Provider changes should preserve component source code for the capability intersection. Unsupported transformations must surface clearly instead of silently changing meaning.</p>\n<p>This is a product hypothesis. It is not evidence of superiority, measured performance, a unique invention, or an absence of competing features. Unpic already solves much of the component/provider problem; enhanced-img and imagetools already solve the common local-image case well. Building a replacement is justified only if the shared lifecycle materially reduces configuration and migration work.</p>\n<h2 id=\"build-or-reuse\">Build or reuse</h2>\n<p>Use Sharp as the Node encoder, not a new image codec. Evaluate existing engines in benchmarks and optional adapters. Keep the URL contract, artifact metadata, security policy, and Svelte presentation independent of any one backend. Do not add ipx or imagetools as a mandatory dependency alongside a duplicate Sharp pipeline: the duplicated transform vocabulary and cache semantics would undermine the single contract.</p>\n<h2 id=\"comparison-evidence-still-required\">Comparison evidence still required</h2>\n<p>No competitor was installed, executed, profiled, audited for security, or benchmarked in Phase 0. M10 must compare current pinned releases on identical licensed fixtures, widths, formats, qualities, concurrency, machines and cache states. Publish raw measurements, failed formats, setup costs, and limits. Do not describe the new package as faster, smaller, or more secure until those measurements support it.</p>\n"
  },
  {
    "slug": "research-package-names",
    "title": "Package name selection",
    "html": "<p>Checked on 2026-10-19 against the public npm registry with <code>npm view</code>, without authentication or publication. The complete responses and exit codes are in <a href=\"/references/docs/research/evidence/npm-view-2026-10-19.json\">registry evidence</a>.</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Candidate</th>\n<th>Registry result</th>\n<th>Assessment</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td><code>svelte-imgkit</code></td>\n<td>E404, no public package found</td>\n<td>Short, but easy to confuse with ImageKit, a provider we intend to support</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>imgkit-svelte</code></td>\n<td>E404, no public package found</td>\n<td>Same ambiguity, less natural ordering</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>svelte-imagekit</code></td>\n<td>E404, no public package found</td>\n<td>Readable, strongest implication of an official ImageKit integration</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>svelte-imageway</code></td>\n<td>E404, no public package found</td>\n<td>Selected: readable and suggests multiple image delivery paths without favoring a vendor</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>svelte-pixelflow</code></td>\n<td>E404, no public package found</td>\n<td>Memorable, less explicit about images and delivery</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<p>The query requested <code>version</code>, <code>dist-tags</code>, <code>engines</code>, <code>peerDependencies</code>, <code>repository</code>, <code>time.modified</code>, and <code>deprecated</code> in JSON. Each name returned a structured registry E404 rather than a network timeout.</p>\n<p>An absent registry entry is evidence of public absence at this time, not a guarantee that npm will accept the name, a trademark clearance, or ownership. Recheck immediately before publication. No scoped package is selected because no npm organization ownership has been supplied.</p>\n<p>The public package is planned as <code>svelte-imageway</code>, including its <code>./edge</code> export. <code>packages/edge</code> will hold the deployable service workspace and stay private initially; this avoids a second public name or an invented organization scope.</p>\n"
  }
];
